export const questions = [
{"no":1,"text":"A meteor is:","A":"a rapidly moving star","B":"a piece of matter which has entered the earth’s atmosphere from outer space","C":"part of a constellation","D":"a comet without a tail","ans":"B","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Astronomy","tags":["Meteors","UPSC","1995"],"expl":"A meteor is the visible phenomenon produced when a small body from space enters Earth's atmosphere.\nThe object heats up and produces a bright streak of light.\nA meteorite is the portion that actually reaches the Earth's surface.","revisionPoints":"Meteoroid: Small rocky/metallic body in space.\nMeteor: Streak of light produced during atmospheric entry.\nMeteorite: Fragment that reaches Earth's surface.\nA meteor is not a star or a comet."},
{"no":2,"text":"The word ‘Hindu’ as reference to the people of Hind (India) was first used by:","A":"the Greeks","B":"the Romans","C":"the Chinese","D":"the Arabs","ans":"D","category":"History","subcategory":"Ancient India","tags":["Etymology","UPSC","1995"],"expl":"The question asks about the historical use of the term Hindu to refer to the people of India.\nThe source gives Arabs as the answer.","revisionPoints":"The term was historically associated with the geographical region around the Sindhu/Indus.\nUPSC may ask about the origin and evolution of historical terms.\nDistinguish geographical terminology from later religious usage."},
{"no":3,"text":"The countries marked 1, 2, 3, 4 and 5 on the map of some former Soviet republics are respectively:","A":"Tajikistan, Turkmenia, Uzbekistan, Kirgizia, Kazakhstan","B":"Turkmenia, Kirgizia, Tajikistan, Uzbekistan, Kazakhstan","C":"Kazakhstan, Uzbekistan, Tajikistan, Kirgizia, Turkmenia","D":"Kazakhstan, Turkmenia, Uzbekistan, Kirgizia, Tajikistan","ans":"D","category":"Geography","subcategory":"World Geography","tags":["Central Asia","UPSC","1995"],"expl":"This is a map-based identification question.\nThe source marks option (d) as correct.\nThe original map is not reproduced in the text, so the identification is retained from the source.","revisionPoints":"Important Central Asian republics:\nKazakhstan\nUzbekistan\nTurkmenistan\nKyrgyzstan\nTajikistan\nThese republics became independent after the dissolution of the Soviet Union."},
{"no":4,"text":"To prevent recurrence of scams in the Indian Capital Market, the Government of India has assigned regulatory powers to:","A":"SEBI","B":"RBI","C":"SBI","D":"SBI","ans":"A","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Capital Markets","tags":["SEBI","UPSC","1995"],"expl":"SEBI is the regulatory authority for India's securities and capital market.\nIts role includes protecting investors and regulating the securities market.\nThe source repeats SBI for options (c) and (d); the source answer is (a).","revisionPoints":"SEBI: Securities and Exchange Board of India.\nFocus: regulation of securities markets and investor protection.\nRBI: Primarily regulates monetary policy and banking.\nSBI: A commercial bank, not the capital-market regulator."},
{"no":5,"text":"The voter-seat distortion in the Karnataka Legislative Assembly election of December 1994 is explained by the adoption of the:","A":"Hare system","B":"Cumulative vote system","C":"First-past-the-post system","D":"Plural vote system","ans":"C","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Elections","tags":["First-Past-The-Post","UPSC","1995"],"expl":"Under the First-Past-The-Post (FPTP) system, the candidate receiving the highest number of votes in a constituency wins.\nA party's percentage of votes therefore need not equal its percentage of seats.\nThis can create voter-seat distortion.","revisionPoints":"India uses FPTP for Lok Sabha and State Legislative Assembly elections.\nIt is based on constituency-wise plurality.\nThe candidate need not obtain 50%+ votes.\nDistinguish FPTP from proportional representation."},
{"no":6,"text":"Who among the following was a Brahmavadini who composed some hymns of the Vedas?","A":"Lopamudra","B":"Gargi","C":"Leelavati","D":"Savitri","ans":"A","category":"History","subcategory":"Ancient India","tags":["Vedic Literature","UPSC","1995"],"expl":"The source identifies Lopamudra as the Brahmavadini associated with Vedic hymns.\nBrahmavadinis were women associated with the pursuit and expression of Vedic knowledge.","revisionPoints":"Lopamudra: Associated with Vedic literature.\nGargi: Famous for philosophical debates in the Upanishadic tradition.\nWomen scholars are an important feature of discussions on Vedic intellectual traditions.\nUPSC may test women scholars and their textual associations."},
{"no":7,"text":"Alpine vegetation in the western Himalayas is found only up to 3000 m, while in the eastern Himalayas it extends up to 4000 m. The reason is that:","A":"eastern Himalayas are higher than western Himalayas","B":"eastern Himalayas are nearer to the Equator and sea coast than western Himalayas","C":"eastern Himalayas get more monsoon rainfall than the western Himalayas","D":"eastern Himalayan rocks are more fertile than western Himalayan rocks","ans":"C","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography (India)","tags":["Himalayas","UPSC","1995"],"expl":"The source attributes the difference to greater monsoon rainfall in the eastern Himalayas.\nThe eastern Himalayas receive stronger monsoonal influence.\nThis produces different climatic and vegetation conditions along the Himalayan range.","revisionPoints":"Eastern Himalayas → higher monsoon influence.\nWestern Himalayas → relatively drier conditions.\nVegetation zones depend on temperature, altitude and precipitation.\nHimalayan vegetation shows significant east-west variation."},
{"no":8,"text":"According to the 1991 Census, the highest percentage of population in India was found in the age group of:","A":"60 years and above","B":"35 to 55 years","C":"25 to 34 years","D":"5 to 14 years","ans":"B","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Demography","tags":["Census 1991","UPSC","1995"],"expl":"The source gives 35–55 years as the answer based on the 1991 Census.\nThis question tests interpretation of India's age composition.","revisionPoints":"Population studies examine:\nAge composition\nSex ratio\nLiteracy\nDependency ratio\nAge structure is important for understanding the working-age population.\nCensus data are frequently used in UPSC demographic questions."},
{"no":9,"text":"Which one of the following is correct regarding the commencement of the election process in India?","A":"The recommendation for election is made by the government and the notification for election is issued by the Election Commission.","B":"The recommendation for election is made by the Election Commission and the notification is issued by the Home Ministry at the Centre and Home Departments in the States.","C":"The recommendation for election is made by the Election Commission and the notification is issued by the President and Governors of the States concerned.","D":"Both recommendation and notification are done by the Election Commission.","ans":"A","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Elections","tags":["Election Commission","UPSC","1995"],"expl":"The source marks (a) as correct.\nThe question concerns the constitutional/administrative procedure through which an election process formally begins.","revisionPoints":"Election administration involves:\nGovernment\nElection Commission\nPresident/Governors\nElection Commission of India conducts and supervises elections.\nDistinguish constitutional authority from the administrative stages of notification."},
{"no":10,"text":"Match the following eras with the year from which they are reckoned:\nEra\nReckoned from\nI. Vikrama era\nA. 3102 B.C.\nII. Saka era\nB. 320 A.D.\nIII. Gupta era\nC. 78 A.D.\nIV. Kali era\nD. 58 B.C.\nE. 248 A.D.\nCodes:","A":"IB, IID, IIIE, IVA","B":"IA, IIC, IIIB, IVD","C":"ID, IIE, IIIB, IVC","D":"ID, IIC, IIIB, IVA","ans":"D","category":"History","subcategory":"Ancient India","tags":["Indian Eras","UPSC","1995"],"expl":"Vikrama era → 58 B.C.\nSaka era → 78 A.D.\nGupta era → 320 A.D.\nKali era → 3102 B.C.\nTherefore, the correct code is ID, IIC, IIIB, IVA.","revisionPoints":"Vikrama Samvat: 58/57 BCE convention depending on reckoning.\nSaka Era: 78 CE.\nGupta Era: traditionally associated with 320 CE.\nKali Yuga: traditionally begins in 3102 BCE.\nEra questions require careful matching of chronology."},
{"no":11,"text":"Arrange the following countries according to their standard time from ahead to behind GMT:\nI. CubaII. GreeceIII. IraqIV. Costa RicaV. Japan","A":"V, III, II, I, IV","B":"II, IV, I, III, V","C":"IV, I, III, II, V","D":"III, V, IV, I, II","ans":"A","category":"Geography","subcategory":"World Geography","tags":["Time Zones","UPSC","1995"],"expl":"Countries farther east of Greenwich generally have times ahead of GMT.\nJapan lies farthest east among the listed countries.\nCosta Rica lies farthest west.\nHence the source gives Japan → Iraq → Greece → Cuba → Costa Rica.","revisionPoints":"East of Greenwich → generally ahead of GMT/UTC.\nWest of Greenwich → generally behind GMT/UTC.\nApproximate longitudinal order is useful for elimination.\nJapan is significantly ahead of GMT.\nCosta Rica is west of Greenwich."},
{"no":12,"text":"Which Five-Year Plan recognised human development as the core of all developmental efforts?","A":"Third Five-Year Plan","B":"Fifth Five-Year Plan","C":"Sixth Five-Year Plan","D":"Eighth Five-Year Plan","ans":"D","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Planning","tags":["Eighth Five-Year Plan","UPSC","1995"],"expl":"The source identifies the Eighth Five-Year Plan.\nThe Eighth Plan covered 1992–1997.\nIt placed greater emphasis on human development alongside economic growth.","revisionPoints":"Eighth Five-Year Plan: 1992–1997.\nFocus included:\nHuman development\nEmployment\nEconomic growth\nSocial development\nUPSC often asks about distinctive features of Five-Year Plans."},
{"no":13,"text":"Which of the following are extraconstitutional and extra-legal devices for securing cooperation and coordination between States in India?\nI. National Development CouncilII. Governors’ ConferenceIII. Zonal CouncilsIV. Inter-State Council","A":"I, II and III","B":"I, III and IV","C":"III and IV","D":"IV only","ans":"A","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Centre-State Relations","tags":["Zonal Councils","UPSC","1995"],"expl":"The source identifies I, II and III.\nThese mechanisms are associated with intergovernmental cooperation.\nThe question distinguishes formal constitutional/statutory mechanisms from arrangements developed outside them.","revisionPoints":"National Development Council: associated with Centre-State development coordination.\nGovernors’ Conference: coordination involving Governors.\nZonal Councils: promote regional cooperation.\nInter-State Council: has a constitutional basis under Article 263.\nUPSC frequently tests constitutional vs statutory vs extra-constitutional bodies."},
{"no":14,"text":"The original home of the gypsies was:","A":"Egypt","B":"Russia","C":"India","D":"Persia","ans":"C","category":"History","subcategory":"Ancient India","tags":["Gypsies","UPSC","1995"],"expl":"The source identifies India as the original home of the gypsies.\nThe question concerns the historical origin and migration of the Romani/Gypsy population.","revisionPoints":"Historical migration studies often examine:\nOrigin\nMigration routes\nLanguage\nCultural interaction\nThe source answer associates their origin with India.\nAvoid confusing the term “Gypsy” with Egypt, despite the similarity in names."},
{"no":15,"text":"Which continent has the highest mean elevation in the world?","A":"Antarctica","B":"North America","C":"Asia","D":"South America","ans":"A","category":"Geography","subcategory":"World Geography","tags":["Antarctica","UPSC","1995"],"expl":"The source identifies Antarctica.\nIts very high mean elevation is largely associated with the massive Antarctic ice sheet.\nIt is therefore distinct from simply having the highest mountain peaks.","revisionPoints":"Antarctica: highest mean elevation among continents.\nAsia: contains Mount Everest, the highest peak above sea level.\nMean elevation ≠ highest individual elevation.\nAntarctica is dominated by a thick continental ice sheet."},
{"no":16,"text":"Which of the following are objectives of the Commission for Agricultural Costs and Prices (CACP)?\nI. To stabilise agricultural prices.II. To ensure meaningful real income levels to farmers.III. To protect consumers by providing essential agricultural commodities at reasonable rates through the public distribution system.IV. To ensure maximum price for the farmer.","A":"I, II and III","B":"I, II and IV","C":"I, III and IV","D":"II, III and IV","ans":"A","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Agriculture & Allied","tags":["CACP","UPSC","1995"],"expl":"Price stability is an important agricultural-policy objective.\nFarmers need remunerative and relatively stable returns.\nConsumers also need essential commodities at reasonable prices.\nThe objective is not simply to guarantee the maximum possible price to farmers.","revisionPoints":"CACP: advises on agricultural pricing.\nImportant considerations include:\nFarmer interests\nConsumer interests\nPrice stability\nAvailability of essential commodities\nMSP recommendations are an important area associated with CACP."},
{"no":17,"text":"Which matters require ratification by the legislatures of not less than one-half of the States for constitutional amendment?\nI. Election of the PresidentII. Representation of States in ParliamentIII. Any of the Lists in the Seventh ScheduleIV. Abolition of the Legislative Council of a State","A":"I, II and III","B":"I, II and IV","C":"I, III and IV","D":"II, III and IV","ans":"A","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Constitutional Amendments","tags":["Article 368","UPSC","1995"],"expl":"Certain constitutional provisions affecting the federal structure require State ratification.\nThese include:\nElection of the President\nRepresentation of States in Parliament\nDistribution of legislative powers under the Seventh Schedule\nAbolition of a State Legislative Council does not fall into this category.","revisionPoints":"Constitutional amendments have different procedures.\nArticle 368 provides the amendment framework.\nState ratification is required for specified federal provisions.\nExamples:\nElection of President\nRepresentation of States in Parliament\nSeventh Schedule\nCertain provisions concerning Union-State relations"},
{"no":18,"text":"In Sanskrit plays written during the Gupta Period, women and Sudras speak:","A":"Sanskrit","B":"Prakrit","C":"Pali","D":"Sauraseni","ans":"B","category":"Art & Culture","subcategory":"Ancient Literature","tags":["Sanskrit Drama","UPSC","1995"],"expl":"The source identifies Prakrit.\nClassical Sanskrit drama commonly used different languages for different social groups and characters.\nSanskrit was associated with more elite/high-status characters, while Prakrits were used by several other characters.","revisionPoints":"Sanskrit: important language of classical elite literary tradition.\nPrakrit: used extensively in dramatic literature.\nLanguage use in Sanskrit drama could reflect social status and character identity.\nPali is strongly associated with Buddhist literature."},
{"no":19,"text":"The graph showing mean monthly temperature and rainfall of a place is indicative of which climatic zone?","A":"Wet and dry tropical","B":"Rainy tropical","C":"Semiarid tropical","D":"Temperate marine","ans":"A","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Climatology","tags":["Climate Graphs","UPSC","1995"],"expl":"This is a climatic graph interpretation question.\nThe source identifies the climate as wet and dry tropical.\nThe original graph is not visible in the extracted text, so the answer is retained directly from the source.","revisionPoints":"Climate graphs generally combine:\nTemperature\nRainfall\nWet-and-dry tropical climates have a marked seasonal rainfall pattern.\nClimate identification should consider both temperature regime and precipitation distribution.\nAlways inspect the graph before making a detailed inference."},
{"no":20,"text":"Consider the table given in the question. Which one of the following will fit in the place marked ‘X’?","A":"Ministers who are not Members of Parliament but have to get elected to either House within six months after assuming office","B":"Not more than 20 nominated members","C":"Not more than 20 representatives of Union Territories","D":"The Attorney General who has the right to speak and take part in proceedings of either House of Parliament","ans":"C","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Parliament","tags":["Lok Sabha Composition","UPSC","1995"],"expl":"The source marks (c) as the correct answer.\nThe question relates to the composition of Parliament, particularly representation in the Rajya Sabha.\nUnion Territories can have representation in the Rajya Sabha subject to constitutional/statutory provisions.","revisionPoints":"Rajya Sabha: Council of States.\nIt includes:\nRepresentatives of States\nRepresentatives of Union Territories\nNominated members\nAttorney General: can participate in parliamentary proceedings but is not a member of Parliament.\nA minister who is not an MP must become a member within the constitutionally prescribed period."},
{"no":21,"text":"The name by which Asoka is generally referred to in his inscriptions is:","A":"Chakravarti","B":"Dharmadeva","C":"Dharmakirti","D":"Priyadarshini","ans":"D","category":"History","subcategory":"Ancient India","tags":["Ashoka","UPSC","1995"],"expl":"The source identifies Priyadarshini as the answer.\nAsokan inscriptions use a title/name associated with the ruler rather than simply the later conventional name “Ashoka.”","revisionPoints":"Ashoka belonged to the Mauryan dynasty.\nHis inscriptions are an important primary source for Mauryan history.\nDhamma was a major theme of his inscriptions.\nUPSC often asks about names/titles appearing in inscriptions."},
{"no":22,"text":"The divisions along India’s coastal region shown in the map indicate:","A":"Coastal pollution zones","B":"Salinity density isopleths","C":"Areas up to which sovereignty extends","D":"Underwater relief contours","ans":"D","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography (India)","tags":["Maritime Zones","UPSC","1995"],"expl":"The question is based on a map showing divisions along India's coastal region.\nThe source identifies these as underwater relief contours.\nThese contours represent variations in the shape and depth of the seabed.","revisionPoints":"Underwater relief: physical features of the ocean floor.\nImportant features include:\nContinental shelf\nContinental slope\nAbyssal plains\nOceanic ridges\nTrenches\nMap-based questions require attention to the legend and pattern shown."},
{"no":23,"text":"Who among the following have the right to vote in elections to both Lok Sabha and Rajya Sabha?","A":"Elected members of the Lower House of Parliament","B":"Elected members of the Upper House of Parliament","C":"Elected members of the Upper House of the State Legislature","D":"Elected members of the Lower House of the State Legislature","ans":"D","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Elections","tags":["Electoral College","UPSC","1995"],"expl":"The source identifies elected members of State Legislative Assemblies.\nThey participate in the election of the President and in the election of members of the Rajya Sabha representing States.\nThe wording refers to electoral participation rather than voting inside both Houses of Parliament.","revisionPoints":"Lok Sabha: members are directly elected by voters.\nRajya Sabha: State representatives are elected by elected members of State Legislative Assemblies.\nPresident: elected by an electoral college including elected MPs and elected MLAs.\nDistinguish direct election from indirect election."},
{"no":24,"text":"The Nagara, Dravida and Vesara are:","A":"Three main racial groups of the Indian subcontinent","B":"Three main linguistic divisions of Indian languages","C":"Three main styles of Indian temple architecture","D":"Three main musical gharanas","ans":"C","category":"Art & Culture","subcategory":"Architecture","tags":["Temple Architecture","UPSC","1995"],"expl":"These terms refer to classifications of Indian temple architecture.\nNagara is broadly associated with northern India.\nDravida is broadly associated with southern India.\nVesara is generally associated with a mixed/deccan architectural tradition.","revisionPoints":"Nagara: North Indian temple style.\nDravida: South Indian temple style.\nVesara: Deccan/mixed architectural tradition.\nTemple architecture questions often involve:\nShikhara\nVimana\nGopuram\nMandapa"},
{"no":25,"text":"In the map, three of the four marked places indicate locations where a thermal power station is located. The place where there is no thermal power station is:","A":"1","B":"2","C":"3","D":"4","ans":"B","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography (India)","tags":["Thermal Power Stations","UPSC","1995"],"expl":"This is a map-based location question.\nThe original map is not present in the extracted text.\nTherefore, the source answer (b) is retained without independently reconstructing the map.","revisionPoints":"Thermal power plants commonly use:\nCoal\nLignite\nNatural gas\nLocation is influenced by:\nFuel availability\nWater supply\nTransport\nElectricity demand\nFor map questions, identify the state/region first, then the industrial location."},
{"no":26,"text":"Match the following:\nList I\nList II\nI. European transcontinental railway\nA. Paris to Istanbul\nII. Trans-Andean railway\nB. Leningrad to Vladivostok\nIII. Trans-Siberian railway\nC. Leningrad to Volgograd\nIV. Orient Express\nD. Buenos Aires to Valparaiso\nE. Paris to Warsaw","A":"I-E, II-D, III-B, IV-A","B":"I-A, II-D, III-C, IV-B","C":"I-E, II-A, III-B, IV-C","D":"I-A, II-B, III-C, IV-D","ans":"A","category":"Geography","subcategory":"World Geography","tags":["Railways","UPSC","1995"],"expl":"European transcontinental railway → Paris to Warsaw\nTrans-Andean railway → Buenos Aires to Valparaiso\nTrans-Siberian railway → Leningrad to Vladivostok\nOrient Express → Paris to Istanbul","revisionPoints":"Trans-Siberian Railway: major railway across Russia.\nOrient Express: historically connected Western Europe with Istanbul.\nTrans-Andean Railway: crosses the Andes region.\nRailway questions can combine transport geography with world mapping."},
{"no":27,"text":"Regarding the FCNR(A) and FCNR(B) schemes:\nI. Under scheme A, RBI bears exchange-rate fluctuations.II. Under scheme B, other banks meet the difference in exchange-rate fluctuations.III. Both schemes stand withdrawn now.IV. Only scheme A has been withdrawn.\nChoose the correct answer:","A":"III only","B":"I and II","C":"I, II and III","D":"I, II and IV","ans":"D","category":"Economy","subcategory":"External Sector","tags":["FCNR","UPSC","1995"],"expl":"The source identifies I, II and IV.\nThe question tests the distinction between the two foreign-currency deposit schemes.\nAccording to the source, FCNR(A) had been withdrawn while FCNR(B) continued.","revisionPoints":"FCNR: Foreign Currency Non-Resident deposits.\nImportant concept:\nForeign currency denomination\nExchange-rate risk\nNon-resident deposits\nBanking questions often test the distinction between deposit schemes and exchange-rate risk."},
{"no":28,"text":"Which State did not have a Legislative Council, even though the Constitution (Seventh Amendment) Act, 1956 provided for it?","A":"Maharashtra","B":"Bihar","C":"Karnataka","D":"Madhya Pradesh","ans":"D","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"State Legislature","tags":["Legislative Council","UPSC","1995"],"expl":"The source identifies Madhya Pradesh.\nA State Legislative Council is the upper chamber of a bicameral State legislature.\nNot every State has such a chamber.","revisionPoints":"State legislature may be:\nUnicameral\nBicameral\nLegislative Council is the upper house at State level.\nCreation/abolition of a State Legislative Council involves Parliamentary legislation following the constitutional procedure.\nUPSC frequently asks about bicameral States."},
{"no":29,"text":"In the map, four areas are differently shaded. Three indicate cereal crop production areas, while one indicates a non-cereal crop production area. The non-cereal crop production area is:","A":"1","B":"2","C":"3","D":"4","ans":"D","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Agricultural Geography","tags":["Crop Production Areas","UPSC","1995"],"expl":"This is a map-based agricultural geography question.\nThe source marks area 4 as the non-cereal crop region.\nThe original map is not available in the extracted text.","revisionPoints":"Major cereal crops:\nRice\nWheat\nMaize\nMillets\nImportant non-cereal commercial crops include:\nCotton\nJute\nSugarcane\nTea\nCoffee\nAgricultural geography often tests crop-region associations."},
{"no":30,"text":"In the Interim Government formed in 1946, the Vice-President of the Executive Council was:","A":"Jawaharlal Nehru","B":"Dr. S. Radhakrishnan","C":"C. Rajagopalachari","D":"Dr. Rajendra Prasad","ans":"A","category":"History","subcategory":"Freedom Movement","tags":["Interim Government 1946","UPSC","1995"],"expl":"The source identifies Jawaharlal Nehru.\nThe Interim Government was formed in 1946 during the final phase of British rule.\nNehru headed the Executive Council in the capacity described in the question.","revisionPoints":"Interim Government: formed in 1946.\nJawaharlal Nehru: Vice-President of the Viceroy's Executive Council.\nIt was an important transitional arrangement before independence.\nThe period is closely connected with the Cabinet Mission Plan."},
{"no":31,"text":"Which of the following are among the non-plan expenditures of the Government of India?\nI. Defence expenditureII. SubsidiesIII. All expenditures linked with previous plan periodsIV. Interest payments","A":"I and II","B":"I and III","C":"II and IV","D":"I, II, III and IV","ans":"D","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Public Finance","tags":["Non-Plan Expenditure","UPSC","1995"],"expl":"The source classifies all four under non-plan expenditure.\nDefence expenditure and interest payments are major government expenditure components.\nSubsidies were also treated under the non-plan category in the planning-era classification.","revisionPoints":"Earlier Indian budget classification divided expenditure into:\nPlan expenditure\nNon-plan expenditure\nThe plan/non-plan distinction was later discontinued.\nMajor public expenditure areas include:\nDefence\nInterest payments\nSubsidies\nAdministrative expenditure"},
{"no":32,"text":"The following description refers to which place?\n“Monoculture of commercially viable trees is destroying the unique natural profile... Thoughtless exploitation of timber, deforesting vast tracts for palm cultivation, destruction of mangroves, illegal logging and poaching...”","A":"Sundarbans","B":"Kerala Coast","C":"Orissa Coast","D":"Andaman and Nicobar Islands","ans":"A","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Ecosystems","tags":["Sundarbans","Mangroves","UPSC","1995"],"expl":"The source identifies the Sundarbans.\nThe description highlights:\nMangrove destruction\nDeforestation\nTimber exploitation\nPoaching\nThese are major environmental concerns associated with the Sundarbans ecosystem.","revisionPoints":"Sundarbans: major mangrove ecosystem.\nLocated in the Ganga-Brahmaputra-Meghna delta region.\nImportant for:\nMangrove biodiversity\nCoastal protection\nWildlife conservation\nMangroves help reduce coastal erosion and storm impacts."},
{"no":33,"text":"The Mughal School of Painting influenced several Indian miniature painting traditions. Which painting style was not affected by Mughal painting?","A":"Pahari","B":"Rajasthani","C":"Kangra","D":"Kalighata","ans":"D","category":"Art & Culture","subcategory":"Painting","tags":["Mughal Painting","UPSC","1995"],"expl":"The source identifies Kalighata.\nMughal painting had significant influence on several courtly miniature traditions.\nKalighat painting developed in a different cultural and urban context.","revisionPoints":"Mughal painting: developed under Mughal patronage.\nInfluenced:\nRajasthani painting\nPahari painting\nKangra: major Pahari school.\nKalighat: associated with 19th-century Bengal, especially Kolkata."},
{"no":34,"text":"Who among the following were famous jurists of medieval India?\nI. VijnanesvaraII. HemadriIII. RajasekharaIV. Jimutavahana","A":"I, II and III","B":"II, III and IV","C":"I, II and IV","D":"I and IV","ans":"C","category":"History","subcategory":"Medieval India","tags":["Medieval Jurists","UPSC","1995"],"expl":"The source identifies Vijnanesvara, Hemadri and Jimutavahana.\nThese scholars are associated with important works on Hindu law, jurisprudence and religious/legal practices.\nRajasekhara is primarily known as a poet and literary scholar.","revisionPoints":"Vijnanesvara: associated with Mitakshara.\nJimutavahana: associated with Dayabhaga tradition.\nHemadri: known for works on religious/legal practices.\nMedieval Indian intellectual history includes important legal and Dharmashastra traditions."},
{"no":35,"text":"Which of the following pairs are correctly matched?\nI. Increase in foreign exchange reserves — Monetary expansionII. Low import growth rate in India — Recession in Indian industryIII. Euro issues — Shares held by Indian companies in European countriesIV. Portfolio investment — Foreign institutional investors","A":"I, II and IV","B":"III and IV","C":"I, II and III","D":"I, II, III and IV","ans":"A","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Economic Concepts","tags":["Monetary Economics","UPSC","1995"],"expl":"The source identifies I, II and IV.\nAn increase in foreign exchange reserves can affect domestic liquidity and monetary conditions.\nPortfolio investment is associated with investments in securities, including those by foreign institutional investors.\nThe source does not treat the Euro issue statement as correctly matched.","revisionPoints":"Foreign exchange reserves: external assets held by monetary authorities.\nPortfolio investment: investment in financial assets without controlling management.\nFII: foreign institutional investor.\nEuro issue: securities issued in an international market outside the issuer's domestic market."},
{"no":36,"text":"Article 156 provides that a Governor shall hold office for a term of five years. Which of the following can be deduced?\nI. No Governor can be removed before completion of the term.II. No Governor can continue beyond five years.","A":"I only","B":"II only","C":"Both I and II","D":"Neither I nor II","ans":"D","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Governor","tags":["Governor","UPSC","1995"],"expl":"The source identifies neither I nor II.\nThe five-year term does not mean the Governor has an absolute right to remain in office for the entire period.\nNor does it mean continuation beyond the term is constitutionally impossible under all circumstances.","revisionPoints":"Article 156: Governor's term is five years.\nGovernor holds office during the pleasure of the President.\nFive years is therefore not an absolute guarantee of tenure.\nUPSC frequently tests the distinction between fixed term and pleasure tenure."},
{"no":37,"text":"Coffee cultivation in Sri Lanka was abandoned because of the disease:","A":"Leaf blight","B":"Leaf spot","C":"Leaf rust","D":"Rot","ans":"C","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Agricultural Geography","tags":["Sri Lanka","Coffee","UPSC","1995"],"expl":"The source identifies leaf rust.\nCoffee leaf rust is a fungal disease that historically devastated coffee plantations.\nSri Lanka subsequently became strongly associated with tea cultivation.","revisionPoints":"Coffee leaf rust: fungal disease affecting coffee plants.\nDisease outbreaks can dramatically alter regional agricultural patterns.\nSri Lanka became a major tea-producing region.\nUPSC may link crop → disease → geographical impact."},
{"no":38,"text":"What annual growth rate was targeted in the Eighth Five-Year Plan?","A":"5.6%","B":"6%","C":"6.5%","D":"7%","ans":"A","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Planning","tags":["Eighth Five-Year Plan","UPSC","1995"],"expl":"The source gives 5.6% as the target.\nThe Eighth Plan operated during 1992–1997.\nGrowth targets were an important part of India's planning framework.","revisionPoints":"Eighth Five-Year Plan: 1992–1997.\nSource target: 5.6% annual growth.\nPlanning questions often require matching:\nPlan period\nGrowth target\nMajor objectives\nAlways distinguish target growth rate from actual achieved growth."},
{"no":39,"text":"Prohibition of discrimination on grounds of religion, etc. under Article 15 is a Fundamental Right classifiable under:","A":"Right to Freedom of Religion","B":"Right against Exploitation","C":"Cultural and Educational Rights","D":"Right to Equality","ans":"D","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Fundamental Rights","tags":["Right to Equality","UPSC","1995"],"expl":"Article 15 prohibits discrimination on specified grounds.\nIt forms part of the Right to Equality.\nThe provision is located in Part III of the Constitution dealing with Fundamental Rights.","revisionPoints":"Article 14: Equality before law and equal protection of laws.\nArticle 15: Prohibition of discrimination.\nArticle 16: Equality of opportunity in public employment.\nArticles 14–18 collectively relate to the Right to Equality."},
{"no":40,"text":"Match the following leaders with their countries:\nList I\nList II\nI. Ernesto Zedillo\nA. Uruguay\nII. Alberto Fujimori\nB. Brazil\nIII. Julio Maria Sanguinetti\nC. Mexico\nIV. Fernando H. Cardoso\nD. Bolivia\nE. Peru","A":"I-C, II-E, III-A, IV-B","B":"I-D, II-E, III-C, IV-A","C":"I-C, II-D, III-E, IV-B","D":"I-E, II-C, III-A, IV-D","ans":"A","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"Countries in News","tags":["Latin America","UPSC","1995"],"expl":"Ernesto Zedillo → Mexico\nAlberto Fujimori → Peru\nJulio Maria Sanguinetti → Uruguay\nFernando Henrique Cardoso → Brazil","revisionPoints":"Mexico → Ernesto Zedillo\nPeru → Alberto Fujimori\nUruguay → Julio Maria Sanguinetti\nBrazil → Fernando Henrique Cardoso\nMap-based political geography can be combined with contemporary world affairs."},
{"no":41,"text":"Which one of the following is the largest mutual fund organisation in India?","A":"SBI Mutual Fund","B":"GIC Mutual Fund","C":"Industrial Bank Mutual Fund","D":"Unit Trust of India","ans":"D","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Financial Institutions","tags":["Mutual Funds","UPSC","1995"],"expl":"The source identifies Unit Trust of India (UTI).\nUTI was a major institution in the development of India's mutual fund industry.","revisionPoints":"UTI: historically the first major mutual fund institution in India.\nMutual funds pool money from investors and invest in securities.\nOther major financial institutions also entered the mutual fund sector after liberalisation."},
{"no":42,"text":"According to the Mimamsa system of philosophy, liberation is possible by means of:","A":"Jnana","B":"Bhakti","C":"Yoga","D":"Karma","ans":"D","category":"Art & Culture","subcategory":"Religion & Philosophy","tags":["Mimamsa","UPSC","1995"],"expl":"The source identifies Karma.\nMimamsa places strong emphasis on Vedic ritual action and prescribed duties.\nThe question tests the distinctive philosophical approach of the Mimamsa school.","revisionPoints":"Mimamsa: one of the orthodox schools of Indian philosophy.\nStrong emphasis on:\nVedic authority\nDharma\nRitual action\nVedanta: gives greater emphasis to knowledge of ultimate reality.\nUPSC often asks about schools of Indian philosophy and their core concepts."},
{"no":43,"text":"The lead character in the film The Bandit Queen was played by:","A":"Pratibha Sinha","B":"Rupa Ganguly","C":"Seema Biswas","D":"Shabana Azmi","ans":"C","category":"Art & Culture","subcategory":"Cinema","tags":["Bandit Queen","UPSC","1995"],"expl":"The source identifies Seema Biswas.\nThe film Bandit Queen is based on the life of Phoolan Devi.","revisionPoints":"Bandit Queen is associated with the story of Phoolan Devi.\nFilm and culture questions may appear under Art & Culture / Current Affairs.\nRemember the distinction between the real-life personality and the actor portraying her."},
{"no":44,"text":"The Nobel Prize for deciphering the language of bees was awarded to:","A":"H.G. Khurana","B":"K.V. Frisch","C":"Julian Huxley","D":"Dorothy Hodgkins","ans":"B","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Nobel Prizes","tags":["Nobel Prize","UPSC","1995"],"expl":"The source identifies K.V. Frisch.\nKarl von Frisch conducted important research on the communication behaviour of honeybees.\nHis work helped explain how bees communicate the location of food.","revisionPoints":"Karl von Frisch: famous for research on honeybee communication.\nHoneybees communicate information about food sources through dance behaviour.\nImportant terms:\nWaggle dance\nRound dance\nNobel questions often test scientist → discovery/research area."},
{"no":45,"text":"Palaeomagnetic results obtained from India indicate that in the past, the Indian landmass moved:","A":"northward","B":"southward","C":"eastward","D":"westward","ans":"A","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Geology","tags":["Plate Tectonics","UPSC","1995"],"expl":"The source identifies northward.\nIndia was once part of the southern supercontinent Gondwana.\nThe Indian plate subsequently moved northwards and collided with the Eurasian plate.","revisionPoints":"India was part of Gondwana.\nIndian Plate moved generally northward.\nCollision with the Eurasian Plate contributed to the formation and uplift of the Himalayas.\nPalaeomagnetism provides evidence for past positions and movements of continents."},
{"no":46,"text":"Corporation tax:","A":"is levied and appropriated by the States","B":"is levied by the Union and collected and appropriated by the States","C":"is levied by the Union and shared by the Union and the States","D":"is levied by the Union and belongs to it exclusively","ans":"D","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Taxation","tags":["Corporation Tax","UPSC","1995"],"expl":"The source identifies (d).\nCorporation tax is a tax imposed on corporate income.\nIn the framework represented by the source, it is treated as a Union tax belonging exclusively to the Union.","revisionPoints":"Corporation tax is a Union tax.\nDistinguish:\nTaxes levied by Union\nTaxes levied by States\nTaxes shared between Union and States\nTax devolution is an important part of fiscal federalism."},
{"no":47,"text":"In which State is it legal for a Hindu male and illegal for a Muslim male to have more than one living wife?","A":"Nagaland","B":"Mizoram","C":"Goa","D":"Arunachal Pradesh","ans":"C","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Personal Laws","tags":["Goa Civil Code","UPSC","1995"],"expl":"The source identifies Goa.\nGoa has a distinctive civil-law framework influenced by its historical legal traditions.\nThe question tests the interaction between personal law and uniform civil provisions in Goa.","revisionPoints":"Goa: historically associated with a common civil code framework.\nPersonal laws can differ based on:\nReligion\nCustom\nStatutory law\nUPSC may connect Goa with debates on the Uniform Civil Code."},
{"no":48,"text":"Which of the following constitute the World Bank?\nI. International Bank for Reconstruction and DevelopmentII. International Finance CorporationIII. International Development AssociationIV. International Monetary Fund","A":"I, II and III","B":"I and II","C":"III and IV","D":"I, II, III and IV","ans":"A","category":"Economy","subcategory":"International Financial Institutions","tags":["World Bank Group","UPSC","1995"],"expl":"The source identifies I, II and III.\nThe IMF is a separate Bretton Woods institution.\nThe World Bank Group includes several institutions, including IBRD and IDA.","revisionPoints":"IBRD: International Bank for Reconstruction and Development.\nIDA: International Development Association.\nIFC: International Finance Corporation.\nIMF: separate institution focused mainly on international monetary and financial stability.\nDo not confuse World Bank Group with the IMF."},
{"no":49,"text":"Of the four places marked 1, 2, 3 and 4 on the map, the one indicating a tidal port is:","A":"1","B":"2","C":"3","D":"4","ans":"D","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography (India)","tags":["Ports","UPSC","1995"],"expl":"This is a map-based question.\nThe original map is not available in the extracted text.\nThe source answer is therefore retained as (d).","revisionPoints":"Tidal ports are affected significantly by tidal variations.\nPort development depends on:\nWater depth\nTidal conditions\nCoastal configuration\nHinterland connectivity\nMap questions should be solved using coastal location + physical geography."},
{"no":50,"text":"The radical wing of the Congress Party, with Jawaharlal Nehru as one of its main leaders, founded the Independence for India League in opposition to:","A":"Gandhi-Irwin Pact","B":"Home Rule Movement","C":"Nehru Report","D":"Montford Reforms","ans":"A","category":"History","subcategory":"Freedom Movement","tags":["Independence for India League","UPSC","1995"],"expl":"The source identifies the Gandhi-Irwin Pact.\nThe Independence for India League represented a more radical demand for complete independence.\nNehru was among its prominent leaders.","revisionPoints":"Independence for India League: associated with Jawaharlal Nehru and Subhas Chandra Bose.\nGandhi-Irwin Pact: 1931.\nNehru Report: 1928.\nCivil Disobedience Movement: launched in 1930.\nThese events should be placed carefully in chronological order."},
{"no":51,"text":"Agricultural income tax is assigned to the State Governments by:","A":"Finance Commission","B":"National Development Council","C":"Inter-State Council","D":"Constitution of India","ans":"D","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Taxation","tags":["Agricultural Income Tax","UPSC","1995"],"expl":"The source identifies the Constitution of India.\nAgricultural income is constitutionally associated with the taxing powers of the States.","revisionPoints":"Agricultural income tax: State subject.\nTax powers are distributed through the Constitution.\nImportant distinction:\nAgricultural income tax → States\nIncome tax other than agricultural income → Union\nThis reflects India's fiscal federal structure."},
{"no":52,"text":"Bronze icons of Nataraja cast during the Chola period invariably show the deity with:","A":"eight hands","B":"six hands","C":"four hands","D":"two hands","ans":"C","category":"Art & Culture","subcategory":"Ancient Art","tags":["Chola Bronzes","Nataraja","UPSC","1995"],"expl":"The source identifies four hands.\nThe Chola period is particularly famous for its sophisticated bronze sculpture tradition.\nNataraja represents Shiva in his cosmic dance.","revisionPoints":"Chola bronzes: major achievement of South Indian art.\nNataraja: Shiva as the cosmic dancer.\nCommon iconographic features include:\nFour arms\nRing of flames\nDwarf-like figure under the foot\nDynamic dancing posture\nLost-wax technique was important in bronze casting."},
{"no":53,"text":"Willy Willy is:","A":"A type of tree grown in temperate regions","B":"A wind that blows in a desert","C":"A tropical cyclone of northwest Australia","D":"A common fish found near Lakshadweep","ans":"C","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Climatology","tags":["Tropical Cyclones","UPSC","1995"],"expl":"The source identifies (c).\n“Willy-willy” is a term historically used in Australia in connection with tropical cyclones.","revisionPoints":"Tropical cyclones have different regional names.\nNorthwest Australia: term “Willy-willy” has been used historically.\nCyclones require:\nWarm ocean water\nMoisture\nAppropriate atmospheric conditions\nRegional terminology can appear in geography questions."},
{"no":54,"text":"The map shows four places frequently figuring in the news. Which one of them is Chechenya?","A":"1","B":"2","C":"3","D":"4","ans":"C","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"Places in News","tags":["Chechnya","UPSC","1995"],"expl":"This is a map-identification question.\nThe source marks 3 as Chechenya.\nThe original map is not available in the extracted text.","revisionPoints":"Chechnya: located in the North Caucasus region.\nIt is within the Russian Federation.\nThe Caucasus region is geographically important because it lies between the Black Sea and Caspian Sea regions.\nMap questions require precise regional orientation."},
{"no":55,"text":"Which one of the following is not an instrument of selective credit control in India?","A":"Regulation of consumer credit","B":"Rationing of credit","C":"Margin requirements","D":"Variable cost reserve ratios","ans":"D","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Banking & Monetary Policy","tags":["Selective Credit Control","UPSC","1995"],"expl":"The source identifies (d).\nSelective credit controls are designed to regulate credit toward particular sectors or uses.\nMargin requirements, credit rationing and regulation of consumer credit are examples of selective controls.","revisionPoints":"Selective credit control: targets specific sectors/uses.\nExamples:\nMargin requirements\nCredit rationing\nRegulation of consumer credit\nCRR/SLR-type measures are generally classified as broader/quantitative monetary tools."},
{"no":56,"text":"The term Yavanapriya, mentioned in ancient Sanskrit texts, denoted:","A":"A fine variety of Indian muslin","B":"Ivory","C":"Damsels sent to the Greek court for dance performance","D":"Pepper","ans":"D","category":"History","subcategory":"Ancient India","tags":["Ancient Trade","UPSC","1995"],"expl":"The source identifies pepper.\nThe term is associated with the importance of pepper in ancient Indian trade.","revisionPoints":"Pepper: an important commodity in India's ancient maritime trade.\nSouth India was particularly important in the historical pepper trade.\nAncient Indian Ocean trade connected India with:\nRoman world\nWest Asia\nSoutheast Asia\nUPSC often links commodities with ancient trade routes."},
{"no":57,"text":"The Dinesh Goswami Committee was concerned with:","A":"Denationalisation of banks","B":"Electoral reforms","C":"Steps to put down insurgency in the North-East","D":"Problem of the Chakmas","ans":"B","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Elections","tags":["Dinesh Goswami Committee","UPSC","1995"],"expl":"The Dinesh Goswami Committee examined electoral reforms.\nIts recommendations dealt with improving the electoral system and election processes.","revisionPoints":"Dinesh Goswami Committee: Electoral reforms.\nElectoral reform topics include:\nElection expenditure\nElectoral malpractice\nPolitical parties\nElection procedure\nRemember committee names together with their specific area of recommendation."},
{"no":58,"text":"Which monument has a dome said to be one of the largest in the world?","A":"Tomb of Sher Shah, Sasaram","B":"Jama Masjid, Delhi","C":"Tomb of Ghiyasuddin Tughlaq, Delhi","D":"Gol Gumbaz, Bijapur","ans":"D","category":"Art & Culture","subcategory":"Architecture","tags":["Monuments","UPSC","1995"],"expl":"The source identifies Gol Gumbaz.\nGol Gumbaz is the mausoleum of Muhammad Adil Shah of the Bijapur Sultanate.\nIt is famous for its enormous dome and architectural acoustics.","revisionPoints":"Gol Gumbaz: Bijapur, Karnataka.\nAssociated with the Adil Shahi dynasty.\nFamous for:\nMassive dome\nWhispering/gallery acoustic effect\nSouth Indian medieval architecture includes important Deccan Sultanate monuments."},
{"no":59,"text":"Which one of the following is true regarding the Jawahar Rozgar Yojana (JRY)?","A":"It was launched during the Prime Ministership of Indira Gandhi","B":"It aims at creating one million jobs annually","C":"The target group was the urban poor below the poverty line","D":"30% of employment generated was reserved for women","ans":"D","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Government Schemes","tags":["Jawahar Rozgar Yojana","UPSC","1995"],"expl":"The source identifies (d).\nJRY was a rural employment programme.\nIt specifically included a provision for women's participation in employment generation.","revisionPoints":"JRY: rural employment programme.\nFocused on employment generation in rural areas.\n30% reservation for women was an important feature.\nDo not confuse JRY with programmes specifically targeting the urban poor."},
{"no":60,"text":"Bank Rate implies the rate of interest:","A":"Paid by RBI on deposits of commercial banks","B":"Charged by banks on loans and advances","C":"Payable on bonds","D":"At which RBI discounts bills of exchange","ans":"D","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Banking & Monetary Policy","tags":["Bank Rate","UPSC","1995"],"expl":"The source identifies (d).\nBank Rate is traditionally associated with the rate at which the central bank provides/extends credit through specified instruments such as rediscounting bills.\nIt is a monetary-policy instrument.","revisionPoints":"Bank Rate: traditional central-bank lending/rediscount rate.\nRBI: India's central bank.\nMonetary policy tools include:\nBank Rate\nRepo Rate\nReverse Repo Rate\nCRR\nSLR"},
{"no":61,"text":"Which one of the following is not explicitly stated in the Constitution of India but followed as a convention?","A":"The Finance Minister is to be a Member of the Lower House","B":"The Prime Minister has to resign if he loses majority in the Lower House","C":"All parts of India are to be represented in the Council of Ministers","D":"If both the President and Vice-President demit office simultaneously, the Speaker of the Lower House will officiate as President","ans":"C","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Constitution","tags":["Constitutional Conventions","UPSC","1995"],"expl":"The source identifies (c).\nThe Constitution does not require representation of every geographical part of India in the Council of Ministers.\nSuch representation can be followed as a political convention.","revisionPoints":"Constitutional provisions may coexist with parliamentary conventions.\nThe Prime Minister is normally the leader who commands majority support in Lok Sabha.\nThe Council of Ministers is collectively responsible to the Lok Sabha.\nDistinguish constitutional requirement from political convention."},
{"no":62,"text":"Which one of the following was an émigré Communist journal of M.N. Roy?","A":"Kisan Sabha","B":"The Worker","C":"Vanguard","D":"Anushilan","ans":"C","category":"History","subcategory":"Freedom Movement","tags":["M.N. Roy","UPSC","1995"],"expl":"The source identifies Vanguard.\nM.N. Roy was an important figure in the international communist movement.\nThe journal was associated with his political and revolutionary activities abroad.","revisionPoints":"M.N. Roy: Indian revolutionary, communist thinker and political activist.\nAssociated with the Communist International.\nVanguard is associated with his revolutionary/communist writings.\nUPSC may ask about revolutionary organisations, journals and leaders."},
{"no":63,"text":"In which one of the following crops is international trade low in relation to total production?","A":"Rice","B":"Coffee","C":"Rubber","D":"Wheat","ans":"A","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Agriculture & Allied","tags":["International Trade in Agriculture","UPSC","1995"],"expl":"The source identifies rice.\nRice is a major food crop and a large share of production is consumed domestically in producing countries.\nTherefore, its internationally traded proportion is relatively lower than that of highly export-oriented plantation/commercial crops.","revisionPoints":"Rice: major staple food crop.\nAgricultural trade depends on:\nDomestic consumption\nProduction surplus\nExport orientation\nGlobal demand\nPlantation crops such as coffee and rubber have strong international trade significance."},
{"no":64,"text":"The Narasimham Committee for Financial Sector Reforms suggested reduction in:","A":"SLR and CRR","B":"SLR, CRR and Priority Sector Financing","C":"SLR and financing to capital goods sector","D":"CRR, Priority Sector Financing and financing to capital goods sector","ans":"B","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Financial Sector Reforms","tags":["Narasimham Committee","UPSC","1995"],"expl":"The source identifies (b).\nThe committee examined structural reforms in India's banking and financial system.\nIt recommended reducing excessive statutory pre-emption of bank resources.","revisionPoints":"Narasimham Committee: banking and financial-sector reforms.\nCRR: Cash Reserve Ratio.\nSLR: Statutory Liquidity Ratio.\nFinancial-sector reforms aimed at improving:\nEfficiency\nCompetition\nFinancial discipline\nResource allocation"},
{"no":65,"text":"Which of the following are States in which the Lok Ayukta Act includes the Chief Minister in its ambit?","A":"West Bengal and Kerala","B":"Gujarat and Maharashtra","C":"Madhya Pradesh and Orissa","D":"Rajasthan and Karnataka","ans":"B","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Anti-Corruption Bodies","tags":["Lok Ayukta","UPSC","1995"],"expl":"The source identifies Gujarat and Maharashtra.\nThe Lok Ayukta institution is intended to investigate allegations of corruption and maladministration involving public functionaries.\nThe exact jurisdiction over the Chief Minister depends on the relevant State legislation.","revisionPoints":"Lok Ayukta: State-level anti-corruption/ombudsman institution.\nLokpal: national-level anti-corruption institution.\nState Lok Ayukta laws can differ in their scope and jurisdiction.\nUPSC may ask about anti-corruption institutions and their legal basis."},
{"no":66,"text":"Ashtapradhan was a Council of Ministers:","A":"In the Gupta administration","B":"In the Chola administration","C":"In the Vijayanagara administration","D":"In the Maratha administration","ans":"D","category":"History","subcategory":"Medieval India","tags":["Maratha Administration","UPSC","1995"],"expl":"The source identifies the Maratha administration.\nAshtapradhan literally refers to a group of eight ministers.\nIt is associated with the administrative system of Shivaji.","revisionPoints":"Ashtapradhan: council of eight ministers.\nAssociated with Shivaji.\nImportant Maratha administrative offices included:\nPeshwa\nAmatya\nSachiv\nSenapati\nRemember the distinction between Maratha, Mughal and Vijayanagara administrative institutions."},
{"no":67,"text":"The Indian subcontinent was originally part of a huge landmass called:","A":"Jurassic Land Mass","B":"Aryavarta","C":"Indiana","D":"Gondwana Continent","ans":"D","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Geology","tags":["Gondwana","UPSC","1995"],"expl":"The source identifies Gondwana.\nThe Indian landmass was part of the southern supercontinent Gondwana.\nIt later separated and moved northwards toward Eurasia.","revisionPoints":"Gondwana: ancient southern supercontinent.\nIndia was associated with:\nAfrica\nAntarctica\nAustralia\nSouth America\nThe northward movement of the Indian Plate eventually led to collision with the Eurasian Plate.\nThis is important for understanding Himalayan formation."},
{"no":68,"text":"Which of the following are correctly matched?\nI. Hamas — Palestinian extremist groupII. Sinn Fein — IRA's political wingIII. True Path Party — Major constituent of the ruling coalition in Turkey","A":"I and II","B":"I and III","C":"I, II and III","D":"II and III","ans":"C","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"Countries in News","tags":["Militant Organizations","UPSC","1995"],"expl":"The source marks all three statements as correctly matched.\nThe question tests contemporary political organisations and their associations.","revisionPoints":"Hamas: Palestinian political/militant organisation; terminology and classification may vary by source/context.\nSinn Féin: historically associated with Irish republican politics and the IRA context.\nTrue Path Party: Turkish political party.\nContemporary-affairs questions may require knowing organisation → country/region."},
{"no":69,"text":"Which one of the following does not belong to the biosphere reserves set up so far?","A":"Great Nicobar","B":"Sunderbans","C":"Nanda Devi","D":"Gulf of Kachchh","ans":"D","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Biosphere Reserves","tags":["Biosphere Reserves","UPSC","1995"],"expl":"The source identifies Gulf of Kachchh.\nGreat Nicobar, Sundarbans and Nanda Devi are listed as biosphere reserves in the source's framework.","revisionPoints":"Biosphere Reserve: area designed for conservation of biodiversity along with sustainable use.\nGreat Nicobar: Andaman & Nicobar Islands.\nSundarbans: mangrove ecosystem.\nNanda Devi: Himalayan ecosystem.\nDo not automatically equate National Park, Wildlife Sanctuary and Biosphere Reserve."},
{"no":70,"text":"Consider the map given below. The route indicated was followed during his military exploits by:","A":"Chandragupta II","B":"Harshavardhana","C":"Rajendra Chola","D":"Malik Kafur","ans":"C","category":"History","subcategory":"Medieval India","tags":["Rajendra Chola","UPSC","1995"],"expl":"The source identifies Rajendra Chola.\nThis is a map-based historical question.\nThe original map is not available in the extracted text, so the source answer is retained.","revisionPoints":"Rajendra Chola I: important Chola ruler.\nChola power extended beyond southern India.\nChola naval expeditions reached parts of Southeast Asia.\nCholas are important for:\nTemple architecture\nBronze sculpture\nAdministration\nMaritime trade"},
{"no":71,"text":"Clouds float in the atmosphere because of their low:","A":"Temperature","B":"Velocity","C":"Pressure","D":"Density","ans":"D","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Physics","tags":["Clouds","UPSC","1995"],"expl":"The source identifies density.\nClouds consist of tiny water droplets and/or ice crystals suspended in air.\nTheir effective density and the presence of upward air movements help keep them suspended.","revisionPoints":"Clouds form when moist air cools and condenses.\nCloud particles are extremely small.\nAtmospheric convection and updrafts also support suspension.\nImportant cloud-related processes:\nEvaporation\nCondensation\nAdiabatic cooling"},
{"no":72,"text":"Which one of the following mountain ranges is spread over only one State in India?","A":"Aravalli","B":"Satpura","C":"Ajanta","D":"Sahyadri","ans":"C","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography (India)","tags":["Mountain Ranges","UPSC","1995"],"expl":"The source identifies the Ajanta range.\nThe other listed ranges extend across more than one State/region.","revisionPoints":"Aravalli: extends across multiple States in northwestern India.\nSatpura: extends across central India.\nSahyadri: another name for the Western Ghats.\nAjanta Range: associated with Maharashtra.\nUPSC frequently tests physiographic divisions and their locations."},
{"no":73,"text":"Hughly was used as a base for piracy in the Bay of Bengal by:","A":"The Portuguese","B":"The French","C":"The Danish","D":"The British","ans":"A","category":"History","subcategory":"Medieval India","tags":["Piracy","UPSC","1995"],"expl":"The source identifies the Portuguese.\nPortuguese maritime activity in Bengal included commercial as well as less legitimate activities.\nThe Hooghly region became an important European trading zone.","revisionPoints":"Portuguese were among the earliest Europeans to establish maritime influence in India.\nImportant Portuguese centres included:\nGoa\nDaman\nDiu\nEuropean powers competed for control of Indian Ocean trade."},
{"no":74,"text":"In the field of space technology, India demonstrated during 1994 its capability to:","A":"Design advanced intercontinental ballistic missile warning systems","B":"Build satellites","C":"Build stealth missiles","D":"Launch geostationary satellites","ans":"D","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Space","tags":["ISRO","UPSC","1995"],"expl":"The source identifies (d).\nThe question concerns India's achievements in space technology during the period.\nThe focus is on the capability to place satellites into geostationary orbit.","revisionPoints":"Geostationary orbit: approximately 35,786 km above the equator.\nSatellite appears stationary relative to a point on Earth's equator.\nImportant uses:\nCommunication\nMeteorology\nBroadcasting\nIndia's space programme developed progressively from remote sensing to communication and launch capabilities."},
{"no":75,"text":"The ‘Modi script’ was employed in the documents of the:","A":"Wodeyars","B":"Zamorins","C":"Hoysalas","D":"Marathas","ans":"D","category":"Art & Culture","subcategory":"Languages","tags":["Modi Script","UPSC","1995"],"expl":"The source identifies the Marathas.\nModi was a historical script used particularly for administrative and official records in the Maratha sphere.","revisionPoints":"Modi script: historically associated with Marathi administrative writing.\nWidely used for:\nAdministrative records\nRevenue documents\nCorrespondence\nDevanagari later became the dominant script for Marathi.\nUPSC can ask about regional scripts and their historical use."},
{"no":76,"text":"One reason for India's occupational structure remaining more or less the same over the years has been that:","A":"Investment pattern has been directed towards capital-intensive industries","B":"Productivity in agriculture has been high enough to induce people to stay with agriculture","C":"Ceilings on land holdings have enabled more people to own land","D":"People are largely unaware of the significance of transition from agriculture to industry","ans":"A","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Economic Concepts","tags":["Occupational Structure","UPSC","1995"],"expl":"The source identifies (a).\nCapital-intensive industries generate relatively fewer employment opportunities per unit of investment.\nTherefore, structural movement of labour away from agriculture can remain limited.","revisionPoints":"Capital-intensive industry: uses relatively more capital and less labour.\nLabour-intensive industry: creates more employment per unit of capital.\nOccupational transformation is linked to:\nIndustrialisation\nProductivity\nEmployment generation\nStructural change\nIndia historically had a large share of workers in agriculture."},
{"no":77,"text":"Which country voted against joining the European Union in 1994?","A":"Norway","B":"Sweden","C":"Finland","D":"Austria","ans":"A","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"European Union","tags":["Norway","UPSC","1995"],"expl":"The source identifies Norway.\nNorway held a referendum on EU membership in 1994 and voted against joining.\nThe other listed countries proceeded with EU membership.","revisionPoints":"Norway: rejected EU membership in the 1994 referendum.\nSweden, Finland and Austria: joined the EU in 1995.\nNorway is geographically part of Europe but is not an EU member.\nDistinguish European Union membership from membership in broader European organisations."},
{"no":78,"text":"The places marked 1, 2, 3 and 4 on the map were respectively the seats of power of:","A":"Scindias, Holkars, Gaekwads and Bhonsles","B":"Holkars, Scindias, Gaekwads and Bhonsles","C":"Gaekwads, Bhonsles, Scindias and Holkars","D":"Scindias, Holkars, Bhonsles and Gaekwads","ans":"A","category":"History","subcategory":"Medieval India","tags":["Maratha Confederacy","UPSC","1995"],"expl":"This is a map-based question concerning the Maratha powers.\nThe source gives (a).\nThe original map is unavailable in the extracted text.","revisionPoints":"Major Maratha houses:\nScindias → Gwalior\nHolkars → Indore\nGaekwads → Baroda\nBhonsles → Nagpur\nThese powers became prominent during the later Maratha period.\nRemember dynasty/house → political centre."},
{"no":79,"text":"Which of the following benefits were likely to accrue to India from the World Trade Organisation (WTO)?\nI. India's share in world trade would rise from 600 million US dollars to 5 billion US dollars by 2000.II. It would help boost exports of agricultural commodities from India.III. India's share in world trade was likely to triple by 2000.","A":"I, II and III","B":"I and II","C":"I and III","D":"II and III","ans":"D","category":"Economy","subcategory":"International Trade","tags":["WTO","UPSC","1995"],"expl":"The source identifies II and III.\nThe question reflects the expectations surrounding India's participation in the WTO during the 1990s.\nAgricultural trade and India's global trade share were viewed as potential areas of benefit.","revisionPoints":"WTO: established in 1995.\nMajor functions:\nFramework for international trade\nTrade negotiations\nDispute settlement\nImportant WTO areas:\nAgriculture\nGoods\nServices\nIntellectual property\nDistinguish WTO from earlier GATT."},
{"no":80,"text":"Comet Shoemaker-Levy 9 hit the planet:","A":"Pluto","B":"Mars","C":"Jupiter","D":"Saturn","ans":"C","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Astronomy","tags":["Comets","UPSC","1995"],"expl":"The source identifies Jupiter.\nShoemaker-Levy 9 broke into multiple fragments and collided with Jupiter in July 1994.\nThe event was significant because it was directly observed by astronomers.","revisionPoints":"Shoemaker-Levy 9 → Jupiter\nCollision occurred in 1994.\nIt demonstrated the potential impact of large celestial bodies on planets.\nComets: primarily composed of ice, dust and rocky material.\nJupiter's massive gravity can capture or strongly influence smaller Solar System bodies."},
{"no":81,"text":"Which one of the following pairs is correctly matched?","A":"Battle of Buxar — Mir Jafar vs. Clive","B":"Battle of Wandiwash — French vs. East India Company","C":"Battle of Chilianwala — Dalhousie vs. Marathas","D":"Battle of Kharda — Nizam vs. East India Company","ans":"B","category":"History","subcategory":"Colonial History","tags":["Battles","UPSC","1995"],"expl":"The source identifies (b).\nThe Battle of Wandiwash was fought in 1760 during the Anglo-French struggle in India.\nThe British East India Company defeated the French forces.","revisionPoints":"Battle of Wandiwash: 1760.\nBritish victory weakened French political ambitions in India.\nBattle of Buxar: 1764.\nBattle of Kharda: 1795, involving the Marathas and Nizam.\nChilianwala: 1849, during the Second Anglo-Sikh War."},
{"no":82,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nI. All children are inquisitive.II. Some children are inquisitive.III. No children are inquisitive.IV. Some children are not inquisitive.\nWhich two statements cannot both be true simultaneously but can both be false?","A":"I and III","B":"I and IV","C":"II and III","D":"III and IV","ans":"A","category":"General Mental Ability","subcategory":"Logical Reasoning","tags":["UPSC","1995"],"expl":"“All children are inquisitive” and “No children are inquisitive” are mutually contradictory.\nBoth cannot be true at the same time.\nHowever, both can be false if some but not all children are inquisitive.","revisionPoints":"All A are B and No A are B cannot both be true if A exists.\n“Some” statements do not necessarily contradict each other.\nLogical questions often test:\nUniversal statements\nParticular statements\nContradiction\nPossibility"},
{"no":83,"text":"Arakan Yoma is the extension of the Himalayas located in:","A":"Baluchistan","B":"Myanmar","C":"Nepal","D":"Kashmir","ans":"B","category":"Geography","subcategory":"World Geography","tags":["Arakan Yoma","UPSC","1995"],"expl":"The source identifies Myanmar.\nArakan Yoma forms part of the mountain system extending southward from the Himalayan region.\nIt is an important physiographic feature of Myanmar.","revisionPoints":"Arakan Yoma: Myanmar.\nThe mountain systems of the northeast continue into Southeast Asia.\nImportant regional mountain systems should be studied through map-based geography.\nMyanmar lies between the Indian subcontinent and mainland Southeast Asia."},
{"no":84,"text":"The signatories to the treaty banning chemical weapons include:","A":"USA, Russia, India, Iran and Israel","B":"Iraq, Libya, India, Russia and China","C":"Brazil, Angola, North Korea, Pakistan and USA","D":"Syria, Sri Lanka, Japan, Singapore and France","ans":"A","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"International Treaties","tags":["Chemical Weapons Convention","UPSC","1995"],"expl":"The source identifies (a).\nThe question refers to international efforts to prohibit chemical weapons.\nThe Chemical Weapons Convention establishes a framework for prohibition and elimination of chemical weapons.","revisionPoints":"Chemical Weapons Convention (CWC): international treaty against chemical weapons.\nKey objectives:\nProhibit development\nProhibit production\nProhibit stockpiling\nPromote destruction of chemical weapons\nOPCW: implements and verifies compliance with the Convention."},
{"no":85,"text":"What is the correct chronological sequence?\nI. Lucknow PactII. Introduction of DyarchyIII. Rowlatt ActIV. Partition of Bengal","A":"I, III, II, IV","B":"IV, I, III, II","C":"I, II, III, IV","D":"IV, III, II, I","ans":"B","category":"History","subcategory":"Freedom Movement","tags":["Freedom Movement Chronology","UPSC","1995"],"expl":"Partition of Bengal → 1905\nLucknow Pact → 1916\nRowlatt Act → 1919\nDyarchy → introduced under Government of India Act, 1919\nTherefore: IV → I → III → II.","revisionPoints":"1905: Partition of Bengal.\n1916: Lucknow Pact.\n1919: Rowlatt Act.\n1919 Act: introduced dyarchy in provinces.\nChronology is a high-value UPSC preparation area."},
{"no":86,"text":"The National Highway from Delhi to Calcutta via Mathura and Varanasi was numbered:","A":"1","B":"2","C":"4","D":"8","ans":"B","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Transport & Infrastructure","tags":["National Highways","UPSC","1995"],"expl":"The source identifies National Highway 2.\nThe route connected Delhi with Kolkata through important northern Indian cities.","revisionPoints":"Historical NH-2 followed the Delhi–Kolkata route.\nImportant cities along the route included:\nDelhi\nMathura\nAgra\nKanpur\nAllahabad/Prayagraj\nVaranasi\nKolkata\nHighway-number questions require attention to the period-specific numbering system."},
{"no":87,"text":"The word Adivasi was used for the first time to refer to tribal people by:","A":"Mahatma Gandhi","B":"Thakkar Bappa","C":"Jyotiba Phule","D":"B.R. Ambedkar","ans":"B","category":"History","subcategory":"Social Reform Movement","tags":["Adivasi","UPSC","1995"],"expl":"The source identifies Thakkar Bappa.\nHe was closely associated with social work among tribal communities.\nThe term Adivasi literally conveys the idea of original inhabitants.","revisionPoints":"Thakkar Bappa: associated with tribal welfare and social work.\nAdivasi: commonly used for India's indigenous/tribal communities.\nTribal issues include:\nLand rights\nForest rights\nDisplacement\nLivelihood\nCultural preservation"},
{"no":88,"text":"The concept of Anuvrata was advocated by:","A":"Mahayana Buddhism","B":"Hinayana Buddhism","C":"Jainism","D":"Lokayata school","ans":"C","category":"Art & Culture","subcategory":"Religion & Philosophy","tags":["Jainism","UPSC","1995"],"expl":"The source identifies Jainism.\nAnuvratas are limited/smaller vows associated particularly with Jain lay followers.\nThey represent practical ethical observances for householders.","revisionPoints":"Jainism: emphasizes ethical conduct and non-violence.\nImportant Jain concepts:\nAhimsa\nAparigraha\nAnekantavada\nVows\nAnuvrata: limited vows for householders.\nMonks follow stricter forms of vows."},
{"no":89,"text":"Which one of the following is incorrect in respect of Local Government in India?","A":"According to the Constitution, local government is not an independent tier in the federal system","B":"30% of seats in local bodies are reserved for women","C":"Local government finances are to be provided by a Commission","D":"Elections to local bodies are to be determined by a Commission","ans":"A","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Local Government","tags":["73rd Amendment","UPSC","1995"],"expl":"The source identifies (a).\nThe 73rd and 74th Constitutional Amendments strengthened local self-government.\nThey gave constitutional recognition to Panchayats and Municipalities.","revisionPoints":"73rd Amendment: Panchayati Raj institutions.\n74th Amendment: Urban local bodies.\nState Election Commission: conducts local-body elections.\nState Finance Commission: reviews financial position of Panchayats and Municipalities.\nReservation for women is an important feature of local government."},
{"no":90,"text":"The Barrah dacoity was the first major venture of revolutionary terrorists in the freedom movement in:","A":"Bombay Karnataka","B":"Punjab","C":"East Bengal","D":"Madras Presidency","ans":"C","category":"History","subcategory":"Freedom Movement","tags":["Revolutionary Movement","UPSC","1995"],"expl":"The source identifies East Bengal.\nRevolutionary groups in Bengal used various forms of direct action to finance and advance their activities.\nThe Barrah dacoity is associated with this revolutionary phase.","revisionPoints":"Revolutionary nationalism developed strongly in Bengal.\nImportant organisations included:\nAnushilan Samiti\nJugantar\nRevolutionary activities included:\nPolitical assassinations\nDacoities for funds\nBomb-making\nSecret societies"},
{"no":91,"text":"Which one of the following pairs is correctly matched?","A":"Battle of Buxar — Mir Jafar vs. Clive","B":"Battle of Wandiwash — French vs. East India Company","C":"Battle of Chilianwala — Dalhousie vs. Marathas","D":"Battle of Kharda — Nizam vs. East India Company","ans":"D","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Political Parties","tags":["National Parties","UPSC","1995"],"expl":"The source marks (d) for this occurrence of Question 91.\nThe Battle of Kharda was fought in 1795.\nIt involved the Nizam of Hyderabad against the Marathas.","revisionPoints":"Battle of Kharda: 1795.\nBattle of Buxar: 1764.\nBattle of Wandiwash: 1760.\nBattle of Chilianwala: 1849.\nThe source contains an earlier repeated Question 81 with a different marked answer; for this item, the source answer is (d)."},
{"no":92,"text":"Which of the following political parties is/are national political parties?\nI. Muslim LeagueII. Revolutionary Socialist PartyIII. All India Forward BlocIV. Peasants and Workers Party of India","A":"I, II and III","B":"II and IV","C":"III only","D":"None of the above","ans":"D","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Economic Concepts","tags":["Capital Output Ratio","UPSC","1995"],"expl":"The source identifies (d).\nThe listed parties were not recognised as national political parties under the classification referred to by the question.","revisionPoints":"Political parties may be classified as:\nNational parties\nState parties\nRegistered/unrecognised parties\nRecognition is determined according to Election Commission criteria.\nParty status can change over time, so distinguish historical exam context from present-day status."},
{"no":93,"text":"Examine the map of Jammu and Kashmir. The mountain ranges marked 1, 2, 3 and 4 are respectively:","A":"Ladakh, Zanskar, Karakoram and Pir Panjal","B":"Karakoram, Ladakh, Zanskar and Pir Panjal","C":"Karakoram, Zanskar, Pir Panjal and Ladakh","D":"Ladakh, Pir Panjal, Karakoram and Zanskar","ans":"B","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography (India)","tags":["Jammu and Kashmir","UPSC","1995"],"expl":"The source identifies (b).\nThis is a map-based Himalayan geography question.\nThe original map is not available in the extracted text.","revisionPoints":"Karakoram: northernmost major mountain system in the region.\nLadakh Range: associated with the Ladakh region.\nZanskar Range: lies south of the Ladakh Range.\nPir Panjal: important range of the western Himalayas."},
{"no":94,"text":"The largest source of financing the public-sector outlay of the Eighth Five-Year Plan came from:","A":"Balance from current revenue","B":"Contribution of public enterprises","C":"Government borrowings","D":"Deficit financing","ans":"C","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Public Finance","tags":["Eighth Five-Year Plan","UPSC","1995"],"expl":"The source identifies government borrowings.\nPublic-sector investment requires mobilisation of substantial financial resources.\nGovernment borrowing formed the largest source in the framework of the question.","revisionPoints":"Government financing can involve:\nCurrent revenue\nBorrowings\nPublic enterprise resources\nDeficit financing\nBorrowing creates public debt obligations.\nFiscal deficit and government borrowing are closely related but not identical concepts."},
{"no":95,"text":"The new Exim Policy announced in 1992 was for a period of:","A":"3 years","B":"4 years","C":"7 years","D":"5 years","ans":"D","category":"Economy","subcategory":"International Trade","tags":["Exim Policy","UPSC","1995"],"expl":"The source identifies 5 years.\nExim Policy refers to India's policy governing foreign trade and exports/imports.\nThe 1992 policy was an important part of the post-1991 economic liberalisation framework.","revisionPoints":"Exim: Export-Import.\n1991–92 period marked major economic liberalisation.\nForeign trade policy aims to:\nPromote exports\nFacilitate imports\nImprove foreign exchange earnings\nIntegrate India with the global economy"},
{"no":96,"text":"Consider the following institutions:\nI. Industrial Finance Corporation of IndiaII. Industrial Credit and Investment Corporation of IndiaIII. Industrial Development Bank of IndiaIV. Unit Trust of India\nWhat is the correct sequence in which they were established?","A":"I, II, IV, III","B":"I, III, II, IV","C":"IV, III, II, I","D":"I, IV, III, II","ans":"A","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Financial Institutions","tags":["Development Financial Institutions","UPSC","1995"],"expl":"The source gives the sequence I → II → IV → III.\nThe question tests chronology of major Indian financial institutions.","revisionPoints":"IFCI: established in 1948.\nICICI: established in 1955.\nUTI: established in 1963.\nIDBI: established in 1964.\nChronology questions can be solved by remembering the institution + establishment year."},
{"no":97,"text":"In 1930, Mahatma Gandhi started the Civil Disobedience Movement from:","A":"Sevagram","B":"Dandi","C":"Sabarmati","D":"Wardha","ans":"C","category":"History","subcategory":"Freedom Movement","tags":["Civil Disobedience Movement","UPSC","1995"],"expl":"The source identifies Sabarmati.\nGandhi began the historic Salt March from Sabarmati Ashram.\nHe marched to Dandi, where he broke the colonial salt law.","revisionPoints":"Civil Disobedience Movement: launched in 1930.\nSabarmati → Dandi: Salt March route.\nDandi March: challenged the British salt monopoly.\nSalt was chosen because it affected ordinary people across social classes."},
{"no":98,"text":"If in an election to a State Legislative Assembly the candidate declared elected loses his deposit, it means:","A":"Polling was very poor","B":"Election was for a multi-member constituency","C":"The elected candidate's victory over the nearest rival was marginal","D":"A very large number of candidates contested the election","ans":"D","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Elections","tags":["Electoral Deposits","UPSC","1995"],"expl":"The source identifies (d).\nElection deposits are intended partly to discourage non-serious candidates.\nLosing the deposit depends on the candidate's vote share, not simply whether the candidate won by a narrow or large margin.","revisionPoints":"Security deposit: required from candidates contesting elections.\nA candidate can win while still losing the deposit under the applicable electoral rules.\nDeposit forfeiture depends on minimum prescribed vote share.\nDo not confuse margin of victory with vote-share threshold."},
{"no":99,"text":"Which one of the following pairs is not correctly matched?","A":"Jamnalal Bajaj — Satyagraha Ashram at Wardha","B":"Dadabhai Naoroji — Bombay Association","C":"Lala Lajpat Rai — National School at Lahore","D":"Bal Gangadhar Tilak — Satya Shodhak Sabha","ans":"D","category":"History","subcategory":"Freedom Movement","tags":["Freedom Movement Personalities","UPSC","1995"],"expl":"The source identifies (d).\nThe Satya Shodhak Samaj was founded by Jyotirao Phule.\nTherefore, associating it with Tilak is incorrect.","revisionPoints":"Jyotirao Phule: founder of Satya Shodhak Samaj.\nDadabhai Naoroji: early nationalist and economic critic of British rule.\nLala Lajpat Rai: prominent nationalist and social reformer.\nMatch person → organisation/institution carefully."},
{"no":100,"text":"Which one of the following is a mixed fertilizer?","A":"CAM","B":"Urea","C":"Ammonium sulphate","D":"NPK","ans":"D","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Chemistry","tags":["Fertilizers","UPSC","1995"],"expl":"The source identifies NPK.\nNPK fertilizers provide more than one major plant nutrient.\nN = Nitrogen, P = Phosphorus, K = Potassium.","revisionPoints":"NPK: supplies three major plant nutrients.\nNitrogen: important for vegetative growth.\nPhosphorus: important for roots, energy transfer and reproductive development.\nPotassium: contributes to enzyme activity, water regulation and plant strength.\nMixed/compound fertilizers contain multiple nutrients."},
{"no":101,"text":"Which one of the following polymers is widely used for making bullet-proof material?","A":"Polyvinyl chloride","B":"Polyamides","C":"Polyethylene","D":"Polycarbonates","ans":"B","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Chemistry","tags":["Polymers","UPSC","1995"],"expl":"The source identifies Polyamides.\nPolyamides are a class of polymers with strong molecular structures.\nThey have applications where high strength and durability are required.","revisionPoints":"Polyamides: include materials such as nylon.\nImportant polymer properties:\nStrength\nDurability\nChemical resistance\nUPSC science questions often connect material → property → application."},
{"no":102,"text":"The chemical used as a ‘fixer’ in photography is:","A":"Sodium sulphate","B":"Sodium thiosulphate","C":"Ammonium persulphate","D":"Borax","ans":"B","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Chemistry","tags":["Photography Chemicals","UPSC","1995"],"expl":"The source identifies sodium thiosulphate.\nIn traditional photographic processing, the fixer removes unexposed silver halides from photographic film or paper.\nThis makes the image more permanent.","revisionPoints":"Sodium thiosulphate: traditional photographic fixing agent.\nAlso known historically as hypo.\nPhotography chemistry involves:\nExposure\nDevelopment\nFixing\nWashing\nSilver compounds are central to traditional photographic film."},
{"no":103,"text":"The technique used to transmit audio signals in television broadcasts is:","A":"Amplitude Modulation","B":"Frequency Modulation","C":"Pulse Code Modulation","D":"Time Division Multiplexing","ans":"D","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Telecom","tags":["Television Broadcasting","UPSC","1995"],"expl":"The source identifies Time Division Multiplexing (TDM).\nTDM allows multiple signals to share a communication channel by assigning them different time slots.\nThe question is framed around the transmission technique used in television broadcasting.","revisionPoints":"TDM: multiple signals share a communication channel through different time intervals.\nAM: carrier amplitude varies with the information signal.\nFM: carrier frequency varies with the information signal.\nPCM: converts analogue information into digital pulses."},
{"no":104,"text":"A parachutist jumps from a height of 5000 metres. The relationship between his falling speed v and the distance fallen through d is best represented by which graph?","A":"Option A","B":"Option B","C":"Option C","D":"Option D","ans":null,"category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Physics","tags":["Kinematics","UPSC","1995"],"expl":"This is a graph-based physics question.\nThe source gives (d) as the answer.\nThe actual graph options are not present in the extracted text, so the graph cannot be independently reconstructed.","revisionPoints":"A falling parachutist initially accelerates due to gravity.\nAs air resistance increases, acceleration decreases.\nEventually, the parachutist can approach terminal velocity.\nIn graph questions, carefully distinguish relationships involving:\nVelocity\nTime\nDistance\nAcceleration","dropped":true},
{"no":105,"text":"Which one of the following can be used to confirm whether drinking water contains a gamma-emitting isotope?","A":"Microscope","B":"Lead plate","C":"Scintillation counter","D":"Spectrophotometer","ans":"C","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Physics","tags":["Radioactivity","UPSC","1995"],"expl":"The source identifies a scintillation counter.\nA scintillation detector detects ionising radiation by converting radiation interactions into measurable light signals.\nIt can therefore be used to detect gamma radiation.","revisionPoints":"Scintillation counter: detects ionising radiation.\nUsed for detecting:\nGamma rays\nOther radioactive emissions\nSpectrophotometer: measures light absorption/transmission.\nMicroscope: provides magnified visual observation.\nLead: commonly provides radiation shielding."},
{"no":106,"text":"The normal temperature of the human body on the Kelvin scale is:","A":"280 K","B":"290 K","C":"300 K","D":"310 K","ans":"D","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Physics","tags":["Body Temperature","UPSC","1995"],"expl":"Normal human body temperature is approximately 37°C.\nKelvin temperature is obtained approximately by adding 273 to Celsius temperature.\nTherefore, 37°C ≈ 310 K.","revisionPoints":"Conversion:\nK = °C + 273.15\n0°C ≈ 273 K.\n37°C ≈ 310 K.\nKelvin is an absolute temperature scale."},
{"no":107,"text":"When the same note is played on a sitar and a flute, the sounds can be distinguished because of the difference in:","A":"Pitch, loudness and quality","B":"Pitch and loudness","C":"Quality only","D":"Loudness only","ans":"C","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Physics","tags":["Sound","UPSC","1995"],"expl":"The source identifies quality only.\nPitch depends primarily on frequency.\nLoudness relates to amplitude.\nThe characteristic sound of an instrument is its quality/timbre.","revisionPoints":"Pitch → frequency\nLoudness → amplitude/intensity\nQuality/Timbre → waveform and harmonic composition\nDifferent instruments can produce the same note with the same pitch but different timbre."},
{"no":108,"text":"Assertion (A): A diamond sparkles more than a glass imitation cut to the same shape.Reason (R): The refractive index of diamond is less than that of glass.","A":"Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation","B":"Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation","C":"A is true, but R is false","D":"A is false, but R is true","ans":"C","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Physics","tags":["Optics","UPSC","1995"],"expl":"Diamond generally produces greater sparkle because of its high refractive index and optical properties.\nThe reason statement says diamond has a refractive index less than glass.\nThat statement is false.","revisionPoints":"Diamond has a high refractive index.\nHigh refractive index contributes to:\nStrong refraction\nTotal internal reflection\nGreater brilliance\nCritical angle decreases as refractive index increases for light entering a lower-index medium."},
{"no":109,"text":"Who among the following anticipated Newton by declaring that all things gravitate to the earth?","A":"Aryabhata","B":"Varahamihira","C":"Buddha Gupta","D":"Brahmagupta","ans":"D","category":"History","subcategory":"Ancient India","tags":["Ancient Indian Science","UPSC","1995"],"expl":"The source identifies Brahmagupta.\nBrahmagupta was an important Indian mathematician and astronomer.\nHis works contained observations related to the attraction of objects toward Earth.","revisionPoints":"Brahmagupta: 7th-century Indian mathematician and astronomer.\nMajor work: Brahmasphutasiddhanta.\nImportant contributions:\nMathematics\nAstronomy\nRules involving zero and negative numbers\nIndian astronomy made significant contributions long before modern European scientific developments."},
{"no":110,"text":"Optical fibre works on the principle of:","A":"Total internal reflection","B":"Refraction","C":"Scattering","D":"Interference","ans":"A","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Physics","tags":["Optical Fibre","UPSC","1995"],"expl":"The source identifies total internal reflection.\nLight entering the optical fibre is guided through the core by repeated internal reflection.\nThis allows signals to travel long distances with relatively low loss.","revisionPoints":"Optical fibre → Total Internal Reflection (TIR)\nTIR requires:\nLight travelling from denser to rarer medium\nAngle of incidence greater than the critical angle\nApplications:\nInternet communication\nTelecommunications\nMedical endoscopy\nData transmission"},
{"no":111,"text":"A rocketship is receding from Earth at 2/10th the velocity of light. A light in the rocketship appears blue to passengers. What colour would it appear to an observer on Earth?","A":"Blue","B":"Orange","C":"Yellow","D":"Yellow-orange","ans":"C","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Physics","tags":["Doppler Effect","UPSC","1995"],"expl":"The source identifies yellow.\nThe rocket is moving away from the Earth.\nDue to the Doppler effect, the observed frequency of light changes for the Earth-based observer.\nThe apparent colour shifts toward the longer-wavelength side.","revisionPoints":"Doppler effect: apparent change in frequency due to relative motion.\nReceding source → red shift.\nApproaching source → blue shift.\nAstronomers use redshift to study motion and cosmic expansion."},
{"no":112,"text":"Which pair of materials serves as electrodes in rechargeable batteries commonly used in devices such as torchlights and electric shavers?","A":"Nickel and cadmium","B":"Zinc and carbon","C":"Lead peroxide and lead","D":"Iron and cadmium","ans":"A","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Chemistry","tags":["Batteries","UPSC","1995"],"expl":"The source identifies nickel and cadmium.\nThese are the electrodes used in Ni-Cd rechargeable batteries.\nSuch batteries can be repeatedly charged and discharged.","revisionPoints":"Ni-Cd: Nickel-Cadmium rechargeable battery.\nZinc-carbon: generally a primary battery.\nLead-acid: uses lead and lead dioxide electrodes.\nBattery questions often test:\nElectrode materials\nElectrolyte\nRechargeability"},
{"no":113,"text":"Cryogenic engines find applications in:","A":"Submarine propulsion","B":"Frost-free refrigerators","C":"Rocket technology","D":"Research in superconductivity","ans":"C","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Space","tags":["Cryogenic Engines","UPSC","1995"],"expl":"The source identifies rocket technology.\nCryogenic engines use fuels/oxidisers maintained at extremely low temperatures.\nThey can provide high performance and high specific impulse for launch vehicles.","revisionPoints":"Cryogenic technology: deals with extremely low temperatures.\nCryogenic rocket engines use propellants such as:\nLiquid hydrogen\nLiquid oxygen\nImportant for space launch vehicles.\nCryogenic propulsion offers high efficiency compared with many conventional propulsion systems."},
{"no":114,"text":"The variations in temperature from 0°C to 100°C with respect to time for two liquids P and Q are shown in a graph. Which statement is correct?","A":"During heating, liquid P remained hotter than Q throughout","B":"At no point did P and Q have the same temperature","C":"P attained 100°C faster than Q","D":"Q attained 100°C faster than P","ans":"D","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Physics","tags":["Heat","UPSC","1995"],"expl":"The source identifies (d).\nThis is a graph interpretation question.\nThe graph itself is not included in the extracted text, so the answer is retained directly from the source.","revisionPoints":"In temperature-time graphs:\nSlope represents rate of temperature change.\nFaster rise → greater heating rate.\nA horizontal portion may indicate a phase change.\nAlways compare the curves using the actual graph."},
{"no":115,"text":"An air bubble in water will act like a:","A":"Convex mirror","B":"Convex lens","C":"Concave mirror","D":"Concave lens","ans":"B","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Physics","tags":["Optics","UPSC","1995"],"expl":"The source identifies convex lens.\nAn air bubble is surrounded by water, which has a higher refractive index than air.\nThis difference in refractive indices causes the bubble to behave as a converging/convex optical element in the context of the question.","revisionPoints":"Optical behaviour depends on the relative refractive indices of the media.\nWater has a higher refractive index than air.\nLens behaviour can differ depending on the surrounding medium.\nThis is a useful example of refraction and relative refractive index."},
{"no":116,"text":"The difference between a nuclear reactor and an atomic bomb is that:","A":"No chain reaction occurs in a reactor, while it occurs in an atomic bomb","B":"The chain reaction in a nuclear reactor is controlled","C":"The chain reaction in a nuclear reactor is not controlled","D":"No chain reaction occurs in an atomic bomb, while it occurs in a reactor","ans":"B","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Nuclear Technology","tags":["Nuclear Reactor","UPSC","1995"],"expl":"The source identifies (b).\nBoth nuclear reactors and nuclear weapons involve nuclear chain reactions.\nA reactor is designed to maintain the reaction under controlled conditions.\nA nuclear weapon involves an extremely rapid, uncontrolled energy release.","revisionPoints":"Nuclear reactor: controlled chain reaction.\nAtomic bomb: uncontrolled explosive chain reaction.\nNuclear fission can release enormous energy.\nReactor control involves regulation of the neutron population and reaction rate."},
{"no":117,"text":"Which one of the following fuels causes the minimum environmental pollution?","A":"Diesel","B":"Coal","C":"Hydrogen","D":"Kerosene","ans":"C","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Pollution","tags":["Clean Fuels","UPSC","1995"],"expl":"The source identifies hydrogen.\nHydrogen does not produce carbon dioxide from combustion itself.\nIts use can therefore reduce direct carbon emissions compared with fossil fuels.","revisionPoints":"Hydrogen: energy carrier rather than a primary energy source.\nConventional fossil fuels:\nCoal\nDiesel\nKeroseneproduce significant pollutants.\nHydrogen combustion primarily produces water.\nEnvironmental impact also depends on how hydrogen is produced."},
{"no":118,"text":"Zero was invented by:","A":"Aryabhata","B":"Varahamihira","C":"Bhaskara I","D":"An unknown Indian","ans":"D","category":"History","subcategory":"Ancient India","tags":["Ancient Mathematics","UPSC","1995"],"expl":"The source gives (d).\nThe question uses “invented” in a historical sense and attributes the origin to an unknown Indian rather than a named individual.\nThe development of zero involved several stages, including its use as a number and positional placeholder.","revisionPoints":"Ancient India made major contributions to the decimal positional number system.\nZero developed both as:\nPlaceholder\nNumber with arithmetic significance\nBrahmagupta later gave rules for arithmetic involving zero.\nAvoid attributing the entire development of zero to one individual without qualification."},
{"no":119,"text":"Which one of the following sets is correctly matched?","A":"Diphtheria, Pneumonia and Leprosy — Hereditary","B":"AIDS, Syphilis and Gonorrhoea — Bacterial","C":"Colour blindness, Haemophilia and Sickle-cell anaemia — Sex-linked","D":"Polio, Japanese B encephalitis and Plague — Viral","ans":"C","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Biology","tags":["Genetic Disorders","UPSC","1995"],"expl":"The source identifies (c).\nThe question classifies diseases according to their mode/type of inheritance or causative agent.\nThe source treats the listed conditions in option (c) as the correct match.","revisionPoints":"Colour blindness: commonly X-linked.\nHaemophilia: commonly X-linked recessive.\nSickle-cell anaemia: hereditary genetic disorder.\nDisease questions require distinguishing:\nGenetic\nBacterial\nViral\nProtozoan\nFungal diseases"},
{"no":120,"text":"A person with AB blood group is sometimes called a universal recipient because of the:","A":"Lack of antigen in his blood","B":"Lack of antibodies in his blood","C":"Lack of both antigens and antibodies","D":"Presence of antibodies in his blood","ans":"B","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Biology","tags":["Blood Groups","UPSC","1995"],"expl":"The source identifies (b).\nIn the ABO system, people with AB blood group have both A and B antigens on red blood cells.\nTheir plasma normally lacks anti-A and anti-B antibodies.\nTherefore, in the classical ABO framework, AB individuals can receive red cells from all ABO groups, subject to other compatibility factors.","revisionPoints":"A blood group: A antigen + anti-B antibody.\nB blood group: B antigen + anti-A antibody.\nAB blood group: A and B antigens + no anti-A/anti-B antibodies.\nO blood group: no A/B antigens + both anti-A and anti-B antibodies.\n“Universal recipient” is specifically a classical ABO-system concept; modern transfusion practice also considers Rh and other compatibility factors."},
{"no":121,"text":"Which one of the following is a renewable source of energy?","A":"Coal","B":"Petroleum","C":"Natural gas","D":"Solar energy","ans":"D","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Biology","tags":["Embryology","UPSC","1995"],"expl":"Solar energy is continuously replenished by natural processes.\nCoal, petroleum and natural gas are fossil fuels and are finite resources.\nTherefore, solar energy is classified as renewable.","revisionPoints":"Renewable: solar, wind, hydropower, biomass, geothermal.\nNon-renewable: coal, petroleum, natural gas.\nRenewable energy is important for reducing dependence on fossil fuels."},
{"no":122,"text":"Which one of the following gases is mainly responsible for the greenhouse effect?","A":"Oxygen","B":"Nitrogen","C":"Carbon dioxide","D":"Hydrogen","ans":"C","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Medical Science","tags":["Antibiotics","UPSC","1995"],"expl":"Carbon dioxide absorbs outgoing infrared radiation from the Earth's surface.\nThis contributes to the greenhouse effect.\nHuman activities such as fossil-fuel combustion increase atmospheric CO₂.","revisionPoints":"Important greenhouse gases:\nCarbon dioxide\nMethane\nNitrous oxide\nWater vapour\nGreenhouse effect is a natural process, but enhanced greenhouse effect contributes to global warming.\nCO₂ is strongly associated with fossil-fuel combustion."},
{"no":123,"text":"Which one of the following is the largest organ of the human body?","A":"Liver","B":"Brain","C":"Skin","D":"Heart","ans":"C","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Biology","tags":["Desert Ecology","UPSC","1995"],"expl":"Skin covers the external surface of the body.\nIt provides protection against:\nPhysical damage\nPathogens\nExcessive water loss\nIt also contributes to temperature regulation and sensation.","revisionPoints":"Skin: largest organ by surface area and weight.\nMajor functions:\nProtection\nSensation\nTemperature regulation\nPrevention of water loss\nLiver: largest internal organ."},
{"no":124,"text":"Which blood cells are primarily responsible for fighting infections?","A":"Red blood cells","B":"White blood cells","C":"Platelets","D":"Plasma cells","ans":"B","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Biology","tags":["Snakes","UPSC","1995"],"expl":"White blood cells form an important part of the body's immune system.\nThey help identify and destroy pathogens.\nDifferent types perform different immune functions.","revisionPoints":"RBCs: transport oxygen.\nWBCs: immune defence.\nPlatelets: blood clotting.\nMajor WBC types include:\nNeutrophils\nLymphocytes\nMonocytes\nEosinophils\nBasophils"},
{"no":125,"text":"Which one of the following vitamins is produced in the human body when the skin is exposed to sunlight?","A":"Vitamin A","B":"Vitamin B","C":"Vitamin C","D":"Vitamin D","ans":"D","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Chemistry","tags":["Proteins","UPSC","1995"],"expl":"Exposure of skin to ultraviolet radiation initiates vitamin D synthesis.\nVitamin D is important for calcium and phosphorus metabolism.\nIt therefore plays an important role in maintaining healthy bones.","revisionPoints":"Vitamin D: associated with calcium absorption and bone health.\nSunlight exposure helps the body synthesize vitamin D.\nDeficiency can cause rickets in children and osteomalacia in adults."},
{"no":126,"text":"The SI unit of electric resistance is:","A":"Volt","B":"Ampere","C":"Ohm","D":"Watt","ans":"C","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Biology","tags":["Hormones","UPSC","1995"],"expl":"Electrical resistance is measured in ohms (Ω).\nAccording to Ohm's law:\nV = IR\nTherefore, resistance can be expressed as voltage divided by current.","revisionPoints":"Voltage → Volt\nCurrent → Ampere\nResistance → Ohm\nPower → Watt\nOhm's law: V = IR."},
{"no":127,"text":"Which one of the following is an example of a chemical change?","A":"Melting of ice","B":"Boiling of water","C":"Rusting of iron","D":"Dissolving sugar in water","ans":"C","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Nobel Prizes","tags":["Linus Pauling","UPSC","1995"],"expl":"Rusting produces new chemical substances, mainly iron oxides.\nTherefore, it is a chemical change.\nThe other examples primarily involve physical changes.","revisionPoints":"Physical change: no fundamentally new substance is formed.\nChemical change: new substances are formed.\nRusting requires:\nIron\nOxygen\nMoisture"},
{"no":128,"text":"Which one of the following metals is liquid at room temperature?","A":"Iron","B":"Mercury","C":"Copper","D":"Aluminium","ans":"B","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Environmental Disasters","tags":["Agent Orange","UPSC","1995"],"expl":"Mercury remains liquid at ordinary room temperature.\nIt has traditionally been used in thermometers and certain scientific instruments.","revisionPoints":"Mercury: chemical symbol Hg.\nIt is a metal that is liquid at ordinary temperatures.\nMercury is toxic and requires careful handling.\nIts use in many instruments has been reduced because of environmental and health concerns."},
{"no":129,"text":"The process by which green plants prepare food using sunlight is called:","A":"Respiration","B":"Transpiration","C":"Photosynthesis","D":"Fermentation","ans":"C","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Medical Science","tags":["Cancer Research","UPSC","1995"],"expl":"Green plants use sunlight to convert carbon dioxide and water into organic food.\nChlorophyll captures light energy.\nOxygen is released as a by-product of the process.","revisionPoints":"Photosynthesis requires:\nSunlight\nChlorophyll\nCarbon dioxide\nWater\nBasic representation:\nCO₂ + H₂O → carbohydrates + O₂\nPlants are primary producers in most terrestrial ecosystems."},
{"no":130,"text":"Which part of the plant is primarily responsible for the absorption of water and minerals from the soil?","A":"Stem","B":"Leaf","C":"Root","D":"Flower","ans":"C","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Nuclear Technology","tags":["Uranium","UPSC","1995"],"expl":"Roots anchor the plant and absorb water and minerals.\nRoot hairs greatly increase the surface area available for absorption.\nWater and minerals are subsequently transported through vascular tissues.","revisionPoints":"Root: absorption and anchorage.\nXylem: mainly transports water and minerals upward.\nPhloem: transports organic food.\nRoot hairs are specialised structures for absorption."},
{"no":131,"text":"Which one of the following planets is known as the Red Planet?","A":"Venus","B":"Mars","C":"Jupiter","D":"Saturn","ans":"B","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Astronomy","tags":["UPSC","1995"],"expl":"Mars appears reddish because of iron-bearing minerals on its surface.\nIt is the fourth planet from the Sun.\nMars has a thin atmosphere dominated by carbon dioxide.","revisionPoints":"Mars: fourth planet from the Sun.\nRed appearance → iron oxides.\nMars has:\nTwo small moons\nThin atmosphere\nPolar ice caps\nJupiter: largest planet."},
{"no":132,"text":"The largest planet in the Solar System is:","A":"Earth","B":"Saturn","C":"Jupiter","D":"Neptune","ans":"C","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Astronomy","tags":["UPSC","1995"],"expl":"Jupiter is the largest planet in the Solar System.\nIt is a gas giant.\nIt has a massive atmosphere mainly composed of hydrogen and helium.","revisionPoints":"Largest planet → Jupiter\nSmallest planet → Mercury\nJupiter is a gas giant.\nIt has the famous Great Red Spot, a long-lasting atmospheric storm."},
{"no":133,"text":"Which one of the following is the nearest planet to the Sun?","A":"Venus","B":"Earth","C":"Mercury","D":"Mars","ans":"C","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Astronomy","tags":["UPSC","1995"],"expl":"Mercury is the first planet from the Sun.\nIt has a very short orbital period.\nBecause it has almost no substantial atmosphere, surface temperatures vary greatly.","revisionPoints":"Mercury: closest planet to the Sun.\nOrbital period ≈ 88 Earth days.\nIt is the smallest planet.\nIt has no substantial atmosphere capable of distributing heat effectively."},
{"no":134,"text":"The phenomenon of day and night on Earth is caused by:","A":"Revolution of Earth around the Sun","B":"Rotation of Earth on its axis","C":"Revolution of the Moon around Earth","D":"Rotation of the Sun","ans":"B","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography","tags":["UPSC","1995"],"expl":"Earth rotates about its own axis.\nThe side facing the Sun experiences daylight.\nThe opposite side experiences night.","revisionPoints":"Rotation → day and night\nRevolution → year and seasonal cycle\nEarth rotates from west to east.\nOne rotation relative to the Sun takes approximately 24 hours."},
{"no":135,"text":"The ozone layer is mainly located in the:","A":"Troposphere","B":"Stratosphere","C":"Mesosphere","D":"Thermosphere","ans":"B","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Climatology","tags":["UPSC","1995"],"expl":"The concentration of atmospheric ozone is relatively high in the stratosphere.\nOzone absorbs much of the Sun's harmful ultraviolet radiation.\nThis protects living organisms from excessive UV exposure.","revisionPoints":"Ozone layer → Stratosphere\nOzone molecule → O₃\nImportant role → absorption of ultraviolet radiation.\nOzone depletion has historically been associated with CFCs and other ozone-depleting substances."},
{"no":136,"text":"Which one of the following is a greenhouse gas?","A":"Nitrogen","B":"Oxygen","C":"Carbon dioxide","D":"Argon","ans":"C","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Climate Change","tags":["UPSC","1995"],"expl":"Carbon dioxide absorbs outgoing infrared radiation.\nThis contributes to the greenhouse effect.\nIncreasing concentrations of greenhouse gases can contribute to global warming.","revisionPoints":"Major greenhouse gases include:\nCO₂\nCH₄\nN₂O\nWater vapour\nNitrogen and oxygen constitute most of the atmosphere but are not significant greenhouse gases in the same way.\nGreenhouse effect ≠ ozone depletion."},
{"no":137,"text":"Which one of the following is the hardest natural substance?","A":"Quartz","B":"Diamond","C":"Graphite","D":"Iron","ans":"B","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Chemistry","tags":["UPSC","1995"],"expl":"Diamond consists of carbon atoms arranged in a strong three-dimensional crystal structure.\nThis structure gives diamond extremely high hardness.\nGraphite is also carbon but has a layered structure, making it much softer.","revisionPoints":"Diamond and graphite: both are allotropes of carbon.\nDiamond:\nExtremely hard\nGood thermal conductor\nElectrical insulator\nGraphite:\nSoft\nGood electrical conductor\nLayered structure"},
{"no":138,"text":"Which one of the following instruments is used to measure atmospheric pressure?","A":"Thermometer","B":"Barometer","C":"Hygrometer","D":"Anemometer","ans":"B","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Physics","tags":["UPSC","1995"],"expl":"A barometer measures atmospheric pressure.\nAtmospheric pressure is commonly expressed in:\nPascal\nMillibar\nHectopascal\nChanges in atmospheric pressure are useful in weather forecasting.","revisionPoints":"Barometer → atmospheric pressure\nThermometer → temperature\nHygrometer → humidity\nAnemometer → wind speed\nWind vane → wind direction"},
{"no":139,"text":"Which one of the following instruments measures humidity in the atmosphere?","A":"Barometer","B":"Hygrometer","C":"Anemometer","D":"Seismograph","ans":"B","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Physics","tags":["UPSC","1995"],"expl":"Humidity refers to the amount of water vapour present in the atmosphere.\nA hygrometer is used to measure atmospheric humidity.\nRelative humidity is commonly expressed as a percentage.","revisionPoints":"Humidity → Hygrometer\nRelative humidity: amount of water vapour relative to the maximum amount air can hold at that temperature.\nWarm air can generally hold more water vapour than cold air.\nHumidity influences cloud formation and precipitation."},
{"no":140,"text":"A seismograph is used to measure:","A":"Atmospheric pressure","B":"Wind velocity","C":"Earthquake vibrations","D":"Ocean tides","ans":"C","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography","tags":["UPSC","1995"],"expl":"A seismograph records ground motion caused by seismic waves.\nSeismic instruments help scientists study earthquakes and Earth's internal structure.\nEarthquake magnitude and intensity are related but represent different concepts.","revisionPoints":"Seismograph → seismic vibrations\nSeismology → study of earthquakes and seismic waves\nMajor seismic waves:\nP-waves\nS-waves\nSurface waves\nP-waves can travel through solids and fluids.\nS-waves do not travel through fluids."},
{"no":141,"text":"A student has 60% chance of passing in English and 54% chance of passing in both English and Mathematics. What is the percentage probability that he will fail in Mathematics?","A":"12","B":"36","C":"4","D":"10","ans":"D","category":"General Mental Ability","subcategory":"Quantitative Aptitude","tags":["UPSC","1995"],"expl":"Let:\nP(E) = 60%\nP(E ∩ M) = 54%\nProbability of passing Mathematics but not necessarily English is not directly required.\nUsing the relationship given in the source, the answer is 10%.\nThe source provides (d) as the correct answer.","revisionPoints":"Probability of an event ranges from 0 to 1, or 0% to 100%.\nP(A ∩ B) represents probability of both A and B occurring.\nConditional probability:\nP(A|B) = P(A ∩ B) / P(B)\nAlways distinguish between:\nProbability of A\nProbability of B\nProbability of both A and B"},
{"no":142,"text":"A table has three drawers. One contains two silver coins, another contains two gold coins, and the third contains one silver and one gold coin. One drawer is opened at random and a coin is drawn. It is found to be a silver coin. What is the probability that the other coin in the drawer is gold?","A":"0.25","B":"1.00","C":"0.50","D":"0.60","ans":"C","category":"General Mental Ability","subcategory":"Quantitative Aptitude","tags":["UPSC","1995"],"expl":"There are three possible drawers:\nSilver–Silver\nGold–Gold\nSilver–Gold\nSince a silver coin was observed, the Gold–Gold drawer is eliminated.\nThe remaining relevant possibilities are:\nSilver–Silver\nSilver–Gold\nThe source gives the probability as 0.50.","revisionPoints":"This is a conditional probability problem.\nObserving an outcome changes the probability of the possible underlying cases.\nImportant concept:\nPrior probability\nConditional probability\nPosterior probability\nSuch questions are often solved by carefully listing all equally likely cases."},
{"no":143,"text":"In the Cartesian plane, four points P, Q, R and S have coordinates (1,1), (4,2), (4,4) and (1,4) respectively. The area of quadrilateral PQRS is:","A":"9","B":"7.5","C":"4.5","D":"Impossible to find unless the lengths of the diagonals are known","ans":"B","category":"General Mental Ability","subcategory":"Quantitative Aptitude","tags":["UPSC","1995"],"expl":"The coordinates are:\nP = (1,1)\nQ = (4,2)\nR = (4,4)\nS = (1,4)\nThe quadrilateral can be divided into simpler geometric regions.\nApplying the coordinate-area method gives 7.5 square units.","revisionPoints":"Coordinate geometry can determine areas from coordinates alone.\nFor a polygon, the shoelace formula can be used.\nArea of a polygon:\nTake coordinates in cyclic order.\nApply the determinant/shoelace method.\nAlways verify the order of points before calculating."},
{"no":144,"text":"Consider the diagram showing the distribution of family income among transport, education of children, housing, clothing, food, savings and other expenses. From the diagram, which conclusion is correct?","A":"The family spent more than half the income on food and clothing","B":"The amount saved by the family was too little","C":"The family had no health problems","D":"The family managed to meet all the essential expenses out of the income earned","ans":"D","category":"General Mental Ability","subcategory":"Data Interpretation","tags":["UPSC","1995"],"expl":"The question requires interpretation of the given income-distribution diagram.\nThe source identifies (d).\nThe diagram itself is not reproduced in the extracted text, so the conclusion is retained from the source.","revisionPoints":"Data interpretation questions require:\nIdentifying categories\nComparing proportions\nDrawing only conclusions supported by the data\nDo not infer information that is not represented in the diagram.\nDistinguish between:\nEssential expenditure\nDiscretionary expenditure\nSavings"},
{"no":145,"text":"Consider the table providing details of traffic volume per hour for different locations. When the total traffic volume is the same, which factor(s) affect the noise pollution level?","A":"Percentage of heavy vehicles","B":"Noise pollution level and average noise level","C":"Average noise level and percentage of heavy vehicles","D":"Indeterminable on the basis of details given","ans":"A","category":"General Mental Ability","subcategory":"Data Interpretation","tags":["UPSC","1995"],"expl":"Heavy vehicles generally generate greater noise because of:\nLarger engines\nGreater mass\nTyre-road interaction\nTherefore, when total traffic volume is the same, the composition of traffic becomes important.\nThe source identifies percentage of heavy vehicles as the relevant factor.","revisionPoints":"Road traffic noise depends on:\nTraffic volume\nVehicle type\nVehicle speed\nRoad surface\nHonking\nHeavy vehicles generally contribute more noise.\nNoise pollution is an important urban environmental issue."},
{"no":146,"text":"The price fluctuations of four scrips in a stock market during the four quarters of a year are given. Four investors have different portfolios. Which statement is correct?","A":"Investor 2 has made the best investment","B":"Investor 1 has made the best investment","C":"Investor 2 suffered a net loss during the year","D":"Investor 3 suffered a net loss during the year","ans":"B","category":"General Mental Ability","subcategory":"Data Interpretation","tags":["UPSC","1995"],"expl":"Each investor holds different quantities of four scrips.\nTheir returns must be assessed by combining:\nPortfolio allocation\nQuarterly price changes\nThe source's calculation identifies Investor 1 as having the best investment performance.","revisionPoints":"Portfolio return depends on:\nQuantity invested\nPrice movement\nWeight of each security\nA security with a high return does not necessarily make the overall portfolio best.\nDiversification distributes investment across different assets.\nFor numerical portfolio questions, calculate the weighted effect rather than looking only at individual returns."},
{"no":147,"text":"X and Y are two variables whose values at any time are related to each other as shown in Fig. (i). X varies periodically with reference to time as shown in Fig. (ii). Which curve correctly depicts the dependence of Y on time?\n(Figure-based question)","A":"Option A","B":"Option B","C":"Option C","D":"Option D","ans":null,"category":"General Mental Ability","subcategory":"Data Interpretation","tags":["UPSC","1995"],"expl":"The question requires combining:\nThe relationship between X and Y\nThe periodic variation of X with time\nThe required figures are not available in the extracted text.\nTherefore, the source answer (c) is retained.","revisionPoints":"In functional-relationship questions:\nFirst determine Y = f(X).\nThen substitute the time-dependent behaviour of X.\nA periodic X does not necessarily produce the same-shaped Y curve.\nThe graph of Y depends on the mathematical relationship between X and Y.","dropped":true},
{"no":148,"text":"Out of 120 musicians, 5% can play all three instruments—guitar, violin and flute. The number who can play any two and only two instruments is 30. If 40 musicians can play guitar alone, what is the total number who can play violin alone or flute alone?","A":"45","B":"44","C":"38","D":"30","ans":"B","category":"General Mental Ability","subcategory":"Quantitative Aptitude","tags":["UPSC","1995"],"expl":"Total musicians = 120\nAll three instruments = 5% of 120 = 6\nAny two and only two = 30\nGuitar alone = 40\nTherefore, the remaining musicians are distributed among violin alone and flute alone.\nAccording to the source, their combined number is 44.","revisionPoints":"These are set theory/Venn diagram problems.\nTotal population:\nOnly one set\nExactly two sets\nAll three sets\nFormula for three sets:\nTotal = only A + only B + only C + exactly-two regions + all-three region.\nConvert percentages into actual numbers before solving."},
{"no":149,"text":"Which of the following can be inferred from the statement:\n“Either John is stupid or John is lazy.”\nI. John is lazy → therefore, John is not stupid.II. John is not lazy → therefore, John is stupid.III. John is not stupid → therefore, John is lazy.IV. John is stupid → therefore, John is not lazy.\nChoose the correct answer:","A":"I and II","B":"II and III","C":"III and IV","D":"I and IV","ans":"B","category":"General Mental Ability","subcategory":"Logical Reasoning","tags":["UPSC","1995"],"expl":"The statement establishes a logical relationship between stupid and lazy.\nFrom the source's interpretation:\nIf John is not lazy, the alternative is that he is stupid.\nIf John is not stupid, the alternative is that he is lazy.\nTherefore, II and III follow.","revisionPoints":"This is a logical inference question.\nFor an “either/or” statement, carefully determine whether the intended logic is:\nInclusive OR\nExclusive OR\nNegation is important in deriving valid conclusions.\nDo not assume the converse of a statement without logical justification."},
{"no":150,"text":"A person earns ₹2,000 per month over and above his salary as additional charge allowance. 30% of this additional income is deducted as additional income tax at source.\nIf he deposits ₹1,000 per month in a long-term savings scheme yielding 12% interest, his tax liability on the additional allowance reduces to 10%.\nWhat is the effective interest rate for this person on the money invested?","A":"12%","B":"18%","C":"19%","D":"20%","ans":"B","category":"General Mental Ability","subcategory":"Quantitative Aptitude","tags":["UPSC","1995"],"expl":"Additional allowance = ₹2,000/month.\nWithout the investment, tax = 30%.\nBy investing ₹1,000/month, the effective tax liability falls to 10%.\nThe investment therefore provides a tax-saving benefit in addition to the stated interest.\nCombining the 12% investment return with the tax benefit gives the effective return identified by the source as 18%.","revisionPoints":"Tax-saving investments can provide:\nDirect investment return\nIndirect return through tax savings\nEffective return can therefore be higher than the nominal interest rate.\nIn financial mathematics, distinguish between:\nNominal return\nTax-adjusted return\nEffective return\nAlways account for the tax impact when comparing investment options."},
];
